var tafsirData_8 = {
  "1": "“They ask you ˹O Prophet˺ regarding the spoils of war. Say, ‘Their distribution is decided by Allah and His Messenger. So be mindful of Allah, settle your affairs, and obey Allah and His Messenger if you are ˹true˺ believers’” opens this chapter about the battle of Badr, the first major encounter between the small community of believers and the Quraysh army. The question, “They ask you regarding the spoils of war,” or “yas’aloonaka ani al-anfal,” concerns the goods taken after battle, a new matter for a community that had just left oppression, embraced succession, and was told the earth belongs to Allah. “Anfal” are additions, surplus beyond the struggle, and the companions differed before revelation about who deserved what, some having pursued the enemy, others guarding the Prophet, others collecting. The answer removes ownership from the fighters and places it with the highest authority: “Their distribution is decided by Allah and His Messenger,” or “qul al-anfalu lillahi wa al-rasool.” Like creation and command belonging to Him alone, the surplus belongs to Him to distribute through His Messenger, who knows best where to place His message and who is grateful.\n\nThe verse then does not immediately detail shares, but first orders the hearts: “So be mindful of Allah, settle your affairs, and obey Allah and His Messenger if you are true believers,” or “fa-ittaqoo Allaha wa aslihoo thata bainakum wa atee‘oo Allaha wa rasoolahu in kuntum mu’mineen.” “Ittaqoo,” be mindful, is the best clothing and the fertile condition; “aslihoo,” make peace, settle between you, addresses the dispute that spoils caused; “atee‘oo,” obey, is the sincere devotion and uprightness commanded earlier. The order shows spoils are a test of the succession, “to see what you will do,” where the material gain that many dispersed groups seized saying “We will be forgiven” now tests whether believers will take the lowly gain again or prove true.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats spoils as entitlement for the brave. The Quran makes them a trust whose decision rests with Allah and His Messenger, whose judgment is the best, as Shu‘ayb said, and whose Word is perfected. For successors who inherited blessed lands and for those who will firm abide by Scripture, the lesson is that even rightful gains are purified by taqwa, islah, and obedience before they are spent, ensuring they become provision enjoyed without waste, like tayyibat for believers, rather than the unlawful gain that inherits Scripture’s price.",
  "2": "“The ˹true˺ believers are only those whose hearts tremble at the remembrance of Allah, whose faith increases when His revelations are recited to them, and who put their trust in their Lord” defines true belief after commanding mindfulness, settling affairs, and obedience regarding spoils. The question about spoils had exposed a dispute among fighters; this verse defines who truly deserves the noble Word of your Lord fulfilled for what they endured, those whose scales will be heavy and who will have elevated ranks. The first mark, “whose hearts tremble at the remembrance of Allah,” or “ithaa thukira Allahu wajilat quloobuhum,” shows a heart alive, like the mountain raised then fearing to fall, like Moses who collapsed when his Lord appeared, like the magicians whose hearts later trusted, not like the hearts turned away even if they see every sign. “Wajilat,” trembling, is the humble secret fear that keeps mercy close.\n\nThe second mark, “whose faith increases when His revelations are recited to them,” or “wa itha tuliyat alaihim ayatuhu zaadathum imana,” echoes “when the Quran is recited listen attentively and be silent so you may be shown mercy” and “We have brought them a Book explained with knowledge as guide and mercy for those who believe.” Recitation increases iman as fertile land produces abundantly, unlike cattle-like hearts that do not understand with what they have. The increase is not mere knowledge but weight on the scale tenfold.\n\nThe third mark, “and who put their trust in their Lord,” or “wa ala Rabbihim yatawakkaloon,” is the trust Shu‘ayb placed, “In Allah we trust, our Lord has encompassed everything,” and Moses’ “Seek Allah’s help and be patient, the earth belongs to Allah.” Trust is the opposite of the chiefs’ reliance on large numbers and arrogance that are of no use.\n\nTogether the three describe the community that will remember when vastly outnumbered and oppressed, then sheltered and strengthened, the same group that earlier invoked morning and evening seeking His Face. For those dividing spoils, the verse says true believers are those whose hearts, hearing, and reliance are moved by remembrance and recitation before they count shares, ensuring the spoils become honourable provision from the Lord rather than lowly gain seized again.",
  "3": "“˹They are˺ those who establish prayer and donate from what We have provided for them” continues the portrait of true believers whose hearts tremble, faith increases, and trust is in the Lord. After the inward marks, the verse gives the outward, consistent deeds that prove belief is not merely feeling. The phrase “those who establish prayer,” or “allatheena yuqeemoona al-salata,” is the same establishment commanded since the call to give just measure, to dress for worship, to enter the city bowing, and to firmly abide by Scripture and establish prayer where reward is never discounted. “Yuqeemoon” means to set up rightly, not merely to perform, as the prayer that is guarded is the light that lets dead hearts walk among people. Prayer is the daily mountain raised like a cloud that must be held firmly with strength, the moderate remembrance morning and evening that prevents heedlessness, and the prostration of angels who are not too proud.\n\nThe second deed, “and donate from what We have provided for them,” or “wa mimma razaqnahum yunfiqoon,” mirrors “Eat from the tayyibat We have provided” and “We have established you on earth with livelihood yet you seldom give thanks,” turning provision into giving. Donation, or “infaq,” includes obligatory zakat and voluntary giving from spoils, harvest dues, and lawful gains, opposite to the inheritance that seizes lowly gain claiming “We will be forgiven” and to Pharaoh’s people who said “This is what we deserve” in prosperity.\n\nTogether the pair, prayer upward and donation outward, is the fertile production by the Lord’s leave, the abundant fruit that shows the Book explained with knowledge has become guide and mercy. They are the qualities of the heavy scale where good is multiplied tenfold, the rivers beneath where bitterness is removed, the heights that hope to enter Paradise.\n\nA common error separates belief from practice, treating trembling hearts as sufficient without establishment. The Quran joins them: those who tremble but do not establish or donate are like infertile land that hears winds but hardly produces. For the companions disputing spoils, the verse teaches that true status is not who collected more but who establishes and donates from what He provided, ensuring the surplus is distributed by Allah and His Messenger for the righteous whose ultimate outcome belongs to those who patiently seek help while the earth remains Allah’s to grant to whom He chooses.",
  "4": "“It is they who are the true believers. They will have elevated ranks, forgiveness, and an honourable provision from their Lord” seals the definition of true believers that began with trembling hearts and faith increasing and trust, and establishment of prayer and donation. The opening, “It is they who are the true believers,” or “ula’ika humu al-mu’minoon haqqa,” uses “haqqa,” truly, in reality, not merely by claim, as earlier many claimed “We have believed” while taking devils as masters thinking they are guided, but true is the one whose deeds match, like the ummah that guides with truth and establishes justice. This true belief contrasts with the “We hear but do not listen” that will be condemned as worst beings deaf and dumb who do not understand.\n\nThe reward is then threefold, each echoing earlier promises. “Elevated ranks,” or “lahum darajaatun inda Rabbihim,” are the ranks according to deeds promised where each has degrees, the degrees the appointed angel Gabriel announced to the magicians and the degrees the mindful attain, unlike the “We will be forgiven” inheritors who chase lowly gain. “Forgiveness,” or “maghfirah,” is the covering the calf-remorseful sought when they cried “If our Lord does not have mercy and forgive we will be losers” and that Moses sought for himself and his brother as the most merciful of the merciful, the forgiveness that pours like perseverance and that removes bitterness from hearts with rivers beneath. “And an honourable provision,” or “wa rizqun kareem,” is the noble sustenance that replaces spoils disputed, the same karim provision that elsewhere is honourable, generous, continuous, unlike the forbidden water to disbelievers in the Fire who beg for any provision, and unlike the seized lowly gain that vanishes as if never there. Its source, “from their Lord,” shows it is granted by the Lord of all worlds who created heavens and earth in six Days and to whom creation and command belong.\n\nThe verse links the Badr spoils dispute to the larger succession test to see what you will do. True believers’ ranks, forgiveness, and karim provision are the hasanah written for this world and the next for those mindful, patient, humble, whose mercy is close. A common misunderstanding measures provision by seized spoils alone. The Quran says the honourable provision from the Lord, given after ranks and forgiveness, is far better than the spoil itself, inviting the fighters to settle affairs with taqwa so the true ranks remain elevated when the weighing is just and the barrier stands.",
  "5": "“Similarly, when your Lord brought you ˹O Prophet˺ out of your home for a just cause, a group of believers was totally against it” connects the spoils question to the expedition that produced them, recalling the march to Badr. The verse says the expedition that led to the spoils was itself a test of the qualities of the true believers. The phrase “when your Lord brought you out of your home for a just cause,” or “kama akhrajaka Rabbuka min baytika bil-haqq,” refers to the Prophet’s departure from Medina toward the caravan and the confrontation that awaited at Badr, and the commentators note the attribution of the departure to the Lord, for the march was not the Prophet’s own venture but a divine commission, the “haqq,” the truth and the right, for which he left his home. Ibn Kathir explains the background: the Prophet had set out with some three hundred and thirteen men to intercept the caravan of the Quraysh returning from Syria, and the expedition, which the believers had expected to be a simple raid upon an unarmed caravan, became the great battle against a fully armed host, and the unexpected turn of events tested the resolve of those who had marched out.\n\nThe verse then states the test: “a group of believers was totally against it,” or “wa inna fareeqan mina al-mu’mineena la-karihoon,” the reluctance of a faction of the believers, the “karihoon,” those who disliked the march and the turn that it had taken, for they had gone out for the caravan and found themselves facing the army, and the fear of the army had made them wish they had not come. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the Quran’s honest record of the human weakness of the believers: the reluctance of a faction was real, and the verse does not conceal it, but the very fact that the reluctance is mentioned is the teaching, for the verse shows that the believers are not angels, and that the test of the faith is the overcoming of the reluctance that the danger provokes. The word “karihoon” is the key: the dislike of the believers was not the rejection of the Prophet but the shrinking of the heart before the danger, and the next verse records how the dispute that the shrinking produced was settled.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the divine arrangement, and the commentators connect it to the beginning of the surah. The surah had opened with the question of the spoils and the definition of the true believers, and the verse now places the expedition to Badr before the believers as the test of that definition, for the believers who had asked about the spoils were the same believers who had shrunk from the march, and the expedition was the furnace in which the claims of the faith were proven. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse teaches the believer the meaning of the trials that the Lord arranges: the departure that was disliked became the victory that was celebrated, and the reluctance of the faction was the first step of the test that the victory would resolve. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the reluctant march: the home left for the truth, the faction shrinking from the danger, and the test of the faith that the expedition was meant to reveal.",
  "6": "“They disputed with you about the truth after it had been made clear, as if they were being driven to death with their eyes wide open” records the dispute of the reluctant faction, and the commentators note the phrase and its force. The verse describes the debate: “They disputed with you about the truth after it had been made clear,” or “yujadiloonaka fi al-haqqi ba‘da ma tabayyana,” the arguing of the faction with the Prophet about the fighting, and the commentators explain that the “truth” here is the fighting itself, the confrontation with the army that the divine arrangement had made inevitable, and the dispute came after the truth had become clear, for the enemy had advanced and the battle was upon them, and there was no longer any room for the choice that the faction had wished to make. Ibn Kathir explains the scene: the believers had marched out expecting the caravan, and when the caravan escaped and the army of the Quraysh appeared, a faction of the believers — those who had not gone out expecting a war — disputed with the Prophet about the wisdom of engaging the enemy, and the dispute is the verse’s subject.\n\nThe verse then paints the state of the disputants with a simile of the Quran’s characteristic vividness: “as if they were being driven to death with their eyes wide open,” or “ka-annama yusaqoona ila al-mawti wa hum yanzuroon.” The image is of the man who is led to his death while he watches, the eyes open and fixed upon the doom that he cannot avert, and the commentators note the force of the simile: the faction’s fear of the battle was so great that the fighting appeared to them as the certain death that they were being driven toward, and their dispute was the plea of the man who sees the death and struggles against the hands that lead him. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the difference between the fear that is felt and the fear that is obeyed: the faction felt the fear, and the fear made the battle appear as death, and the dispute was the expression of the fear, but the truth had been made clear, and the clarity of the truth is the answer to the fear, for the one who knows that the matter is the truth no longer debates it, whatever the fear may paint.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the dispute that the fear breeds, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hesitation. The dispute about the truth after its clarity is the mark of the heart that has not yet submitted, for the truth, once clear, is not a matter of debate but of obedience, and the one who continues to argue with the clear command is the one whose fear has not yet been mastered by the faith. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the Quran’s warning against the arguing that follows the clarity, and that the believer is taught to close the debate when the truth has been shown, for the arguing after the clarity is the first step of the retreat that the verse’s simile foreshadows. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the dispute: the truth made clear, the faction arguing, and the fear that made the battle appear as death — the warning against the debate that the clear command should have silenced.",
  "7": "“˹Remember, O believers,˺ when Allah promised ˹to give˺ you the upper hand over either target, you wished to capture the unarmed party. But it was Allah’s Will to establish the truth by His Words and uproot the disbelievers” records the divine promise before Badr and the preference of the believers, and the commentators explain the two targets. The verse recalls the promise: “when Allah promised to give you the upper hand over either target,” or “wa ith ya‘idukumu Allahu ihda at-ta’ifatayni anna lakum,” the promise that God had made to the believers of victory over one of the two groups — the caravan or the army — and the commentators identify the two: the caravan of Abu Sufyan, the lightly guarded trading party returning from Syria, and the army of the Quraysh, the fully armed host that had marched out from Mecca to protect the caravan. Ibn Kathir explains the preference of the believers: “you wished to capture the unarmed party,” or “wa tawaddoona anna ghayra thati ash-shawkati takoonu lakum,” the believers wished for the caravan, the party without the “shawkah,” the armed strength, for the caravan promised the easy spoil and the bloodless gain, and the natural preference of the human heart is for the gain without the danger, the victory without the battle.\n\nThe verse then states the divine will that overruled the preference: “But it was Allah’s Will to establish the truth by His Words and uproot the disbelievers,” or “wa yureedu Allahu an yuhiqqa al-haqqa bi-kalimatihi wa yaqta‘a dabira al-kafireen.” The commentators dwell on this sentence, for it states the purpose of the divine arrangement: God willed the confrontation with the army, not the capture of the caravan, because the establishment of the truth and the uprooting of the disbelievers required the great battle, the decisive encounter that would demonstrate the power of the truth against the might of the falsehood, and the victory over the caravan would have achieved none of it. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the difference between the human wish and the divine will: the believers wished for the easy, and God willed the decisive, and the decisive was the mercy that the easy would have denied, for the battle of Badr became the sign that established the truth in the land, while the capture of the caravan would have left the truth unproven. The verse is the Quran’s teaching that the believer’s preference is not the measure of the divine arrangement, and that the Lord wills for His servants the good that their wishes often fail to see.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the two targets, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own choices. The believer is always faced with the two targets — the easy gain and the hard duty — and the heart naturally prefers the easy, and the verse teaches that the divine wisdom often assigns the hard, for the hard is the means of the establishment of the truth, and the one who trusts the Lord accepts the assignment of the hard as the mercy that the easy could not supply. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the prelude to the account of Badr, and that the believer reads in it the principle that governs every divine arrangement: the will of God is for the truth and its establishment, and the preference of the servant is for the ease and its gain, and the two meet in the test that the victory resolves. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the two targets: the caravan wished, the army willed, and the purpose of the will declared — the establishment of the truth and the uprooting of the falsehood, the divine end that the human wish could not see.",
  "8": "“to firmly establish the truth and wipe out falsehood—even to the dismay of the wicked” completes the purpose clause of the divine will, and the commentators note the two ends and the closing phrase. The verse continues the statement of the purpose: “to firmly establish the truth and wipe out falsehood,” or “li-yuhiqqa al-haqqa wa yubtila al-batila,” the two ends of the divine arrangement, the establishment of the truth, the “ihqaq,” the making of the truth firm and manifest, and the wiping out of the falsehood, the “ibtal,” the rendering of the falsehood vain and null, and the two are the single purpose of the battle of Badr, for the truth and the falsehood cannot dwell together, and the establishment of the one is the abolition of the other. Ibn Kathir explains the pairing: the victory at Badr was not a mere military success but the divine act by which the truth was established in the land of Arabia and the falsehood of the idolaters was wiped out, and the two words — “yuhiqqa” and “yubtila” — are the two sides of the single verdict that the battle pronounced.\n\nThe verse closes with the phrase that seals the purpose: “even to the dismay of the wicked,” or “wa law kariha al-mujrimoon.” The establishment of the truth and the wiping out of the falsehood are willed by God regardless of the dislike of the wicked, the “mujrimoon,” the criminals, the disbelievers who hate the truth and its triumph, and the phrase teaches that the divine purpose does not consult the wishes of the falsehood, for the truth is established and the falsehood is abolished whether the wicked like it or not. Al-Sa’di observes that the phrase is the assurance of the believer and the warning of the denier: the believer is told that the truth will prevail despite the hatred of its enemies, and the denier is told that the falsehood will be wiped out despite his love of it, and the dismay of the wicked is the measure of the triumph of the truth. The verse is the statement of the inevitability of the divine purpose, the declaration that the battle between the truth and the falsehood has an outcome that the wicked cannot avert.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the two verdicts, and the commentators connect them to the believer’s confidence. The believer who knows that God wills the establishment of the truth and the wiping out of the falsehood fights with the confidence of the one whose side is already chosen by the divine will, and the dismay of the wicked is the assurance that the hatred of the enemies is not the obstacle to the victory but the sign of it, for the truth that the wicked hate is the truth that God will establish. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the completion of the purpose clause of the previous verse, and that the believer reads in it the ground of his hope: the truth is established and the falsehood is wiped out by the will of God, and the dismay of the wicked is the tribute that the falsehood pays to the truth it cannot stop. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the divine purpose: the truth established, the falsehood wiped out, and the dismay of the wicked — the inevitability of the verdict that the battle of Badr pronounced.",
  "9": "“˹Remember˺ when you cried out to your Lord for help, He answered, ‘I will reinforce you with a thousand angels—followed by many others’” records the prayer of the Prophet and the answer, and the commentators describe the scene and the promise. The verse recalls the moment of the prayer: “when you cried out to your Lord for help,” or “ith tastagheethoona rabbakum,” the crying of the believers to their Lord for the aid, and the commentators note that the “you” is the plural of the believers or the Prophet as their representative, and the prayer was the turning of the outnumbered few to the only power that could help them. Ibn Kathir describes the scene: the believers at Badr looked upon the army of the Quraysh, a thousand strong and fully armed, and the Prophet raised his hands and prayed with the urgency that the hour demanded, saying, “O Allah, if this band perishes today, You will not be worshipped on the earth,” and the prayer was the cry of the one who had done all that a man could do and turned the rest to his Lord.\n\nThe answer is then recorded: “He answered, ‘I will reinforce you with a thousand angels—followed by many others,’” or “fastajaba lakum annee mumiddukum bi-alfin mina al-mala’ikati murdifeen.” The promise of the thousand angels, the reinforcement that the believers could not have hoped for, and the word “murdifeen,” the ones following in succession, the angels arriving wave after wave, and the commentators note the generosity of the answer: the believers had cried out, and the Lord answered with the hosts of the heaven, the thousand angels that the verses of the battle elsewhere describe as descending upon the field. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the power of the prayer in the hour of the need: the cry of the outnumbered was answered by the legions of the unseen, and the answer is the proof that the believer is never so few that the help of his Lord cannot make him many, for the thousand angels are the measure of the help that descends upon the cry of the sincere. The verse is the record of the answered prayer, and the promise that the cry of the believer is never lost.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reinforcement, and the commentators connect it to the theology of the divine aid. The angels were sent not because the believers were insufficient but because the Lord honours His servants with the aid that exceeds their own strength, and the promise of the thousand is the assurance that the help of God is proportioned to the need of the believer, the thousand for the outnumbered, and the many for the few. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the first of the descriptions of the angelic aid at Badr, and that the believer reads in it the source of the confidence that no numerical disparity can shake: the one who cries to his Lord in the hour of the need is the one who is reinforced by the hosts of the heaven. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the answered cry: the prayer raised, the thousand promised, and the angels following in succession — the help of the Lord descending upon the outnumbered few.",
  "10": "“And Allah made this a sign of victory and reassurance to your hearts. Victory comes only from Allah. Surely Allah is Almighty, All-Wise” explains the purpose of the angelic reinforcement and states the source of the victory, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first part is the purpose: “And Allah made this a sign of victory and reassurance to your hearts,” or “wa ma ja‘alahu Allahu illa bushra wa li-tatma’inna bihi qulubukum,” the reinforcement of the angels was made a “bushra,” a glad tiding, a sign of the victory, and a reassurance for the hearts, the “itmi’nan” that calms the fear, and the commentators note that the purpose of the angels was inward before it was outward, for the real battle of Badr was fought first in the hearts of the believers, and the knowledge of the angelic host was the medicine for the fear that the hearts had felt. Ibn Kathir explains the meaning: the angels did not come because the believers could not fight but because the believers needed to know that they did not fight alone, and the sign of the angels was the reassurance that the Lord had not abandoned them, the visible proof that the heaven was with the earth.\n\nThe second part is the source: “Victory comes only from Allah. Surely Allah is Almighty, All-Wise,” or “wa ma an-nasru illa min ‘indi Allahi inna Allaha ‘azeezun hakeem.” The word “illa,” only, is absolute, and the commentators dwell on it: the victory is not from the numbers, the weapons, the strategy, or even the angels, but from God alone, and the angels themselves are but the servants of the victory, the instruments of the One who grants it. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the correction of every reliance upon the visible means: the believers were shown the angels so that their hearts might be reassured, and then they were told that the victory is from God alone, so that the reassurance might rest not upon the angels but upon the One who sent them, and the two movements — the sign for the heart and the source for the faith — are the complete teaching of the verse. The names “al-‘Aziz,” the Almighty, and “al-Hakim,” the All-Wise, seal the statement: the power that grants the victory and the wisdom that apportions it, and the believer rests in both.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the order of the reassurance and the source, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is given the signs that reassure the heart — the provisions, the helpers, the favourable circumstances — and then the believer is taught that the signs are not the source, for the source is God alone, and the heart that rests upon the sign without the Source has rested upon the servant, while the heart that rests upon the Source through the sign has found the ground that no sign can shake. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the companion of the previous verse, and that the believer reads in it the complete theology of the victory: the angels for the heart, and the Lord for the victory, and the two joined in the single truth that the Almighty, the All-Wise is the only source of the help. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the source: the sign given, the heart reassured, and the victory declared from God alone — the Almighty, the All-Wise, the only source of the help.",
  "11": "“˹Remember˺ when He caused drowsiness to overcome you, giving you serenity. And He sent down rain from the sky to purify you, free you from Satan’s whispers, strengthen your hearts, and make ˹your˺ steps firm” records the two gifts of the night before Badr, and the commentators describe both with the detail that the traditions supply. The first gift is the sleep: “when He caused drowsiness to overcome you, giving you serenity,” or “ith yughashsheekumu an-nu‘asa amanatan minhu,” the drowsiness that God cast over the believers on the night before the battle, the sleep that fell upon them in the face of the enemy, and the commentators note that the sleep was the “amanah,” the security, the serenity that the hearts needed, for the man who sleeps soundly on the eve of the battle is the man whose heart is at peace, and the sleep of the believers was the sign of the peace that God had granted them. Ibn Kathir relates the scene: the believers, exhausted and anxious, were overtaken by the sleep that God sent down upon them, and they slept so soundly that the morning found them refreshed and their fear calmed, and the sleep was the first of the gifts of the night.\n\nThe second gift is the rain: “And He sent down rain from the sky to purify you, free you from Satan’s whispers, strengthen your hearts, and make your steps firm,” or “wa yunazzilu ‘alaykum mina as-sama’i ma’an li-yutahhirakum bihi wa yuthhiba ‘ankum rijza ash-shaytani wa li-yarbita ‘ala quloobikum wa yuthabbita bihi al-aqdam.” The commentators explain the four purposes of the rain: the purification, the washing of the believers from the impurity and the preparation for the prayer; the removal of the “rijz” of Satan, the whispers and the doubts that the enemy plants in the heart; the strengthening of the hearts, the “rabt,” the binding of the courage that the rain revived; and the firming of the steps, the “tathbit” of the feet upon the ground, for the rain had fallen upon the sandy ground and made it firm under the feet of the believers while the ground of the enemy remained soft and treacherous. Al-Sa’di observes the completeness of the rain’s work: the single shower served the body, the heart, and the ground at once, the purification and the courage and the footing, and the four purposes are the four needs of the soldier that the Lord supplied in a single gift.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the divine care in the details, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s trust. The Lord who arranged the sleep and the rain for the believers at Badr is the Lord who arranges the details of the believer’s own battles, and the verse teaches that the help of God often comes in the small things — the sleep that calms, the rain that firms the ground — the unnoticed mercies that the believer receives and the heedless never see. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the record of the divine care for the army of the believers, and that the believer reads in it the assurance that the Lord who is with him in the great things is with him in the small, and that the sleep and the rain of Badr are the signs of the care that never sleeps. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the night before the battle: the sleep that gave the serenity, and the rain that purified and strengthened and firmed — the care of the Lord for the army of the faithful.",
  "12": "“˹Remember, O Prophet,˺ when your Lord revealed to the angels, ‘I am with you. So make the believers stand firm. I will cast horror into the hearts of the disbelievers. So strike their necks and strike their fingertips’” records the revelation to the angels, and the commentators note the four commands that it contains. The verse recalls the address of the Lord to the angelic host: “when your Lord revealed to the angels,” or “ith yoohee rabbuka ila al-mala’ikati,” the inspiration sent to the angels on the day of the battle, and the content of the revelation is the fourfold command that the verse preserves. The first is the assurance: “I am with you,” or “annee ma‘akum,” the declaration of the divine presence with the angels, the assurance that the heavenly host fought under the eye of the One who had sent them, and the commentators note that the phrase is the ground of all that follows, for the angels who know that their Lord is with them fight with the strength of the certainty. Ibn Kathir explains the command to the angels: they were sent to fight at the side of the believers, and the revelation directed their action in the battle.\n\nThe second command is directed to the believers through the angels: “So make the believers stand firm,” or “fa-thabbitoo allatheena amanoo,” the strengthening of the believers, the “tathbit” that the angels performed by their presence and their aid, the steadying of the hearts and the feet of the faithful in the clash. The third is the casting of the fear into the enemy: “I will cast horror into the hearts of the disbelievers,” or “sa-ulqee fi quloobi allatheena kafaroo ar-ru‘ba,” the terror that God cast into the hearts of the Quraysh, the “ru‘b,” the panic that seized the enemy and broke their ranks before the battle was decided. The fourth is the command to strike: “So strike their necks and strike their fingertips,” or “fadriboo fawqa al-a‘naqi wadriboo minhum kulla banan,” the striking of the necks and the fingertips, the two targets that the verse names, and the commentators explain the detail: the neck is the seat of the life, and the fingertips are the instruments of the sword, and the striking of the two is the complete disabling of the enemy, the severing of the life and the hand that held the weapon. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse describes the full participation of the angels in the battle, and that the four commands — the presence, the firming, the terror, and the striking — are the four works of the heavenly host upon the field of Badr.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the unseen aid, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s confidence. The battle of Badr was not fought by the three hundred and thirteen alone, and the verse reveals the host that fought at their side, the angels whom the eye of the believer could not see and the eye of the enemy could not bear, and the revelation to the angels is the assurance that the believer’s struggles are joined by the forces of the unseen that the Lord commands. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the description of the angelic participation in the battle, and that the believer reads in it the truth of the unseen aid: the Lord who revealed to the angels is the Lord who aids the believer, and the commands to the angels are the commands that the believer’s own steadfastness invites. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the heavenly host: the presence declared, the believers firmed, the terror cast, and the striking commanded — the revelation to the angels that fought at the side of the faithful.",
  "13": "“This is because they defied Allah and His Messenger. And whoever defies Allah and His Messenger, then ˹know that˺ Allah is surely severe in punishment” states the cause of the striking and the principle that governs it, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The first is the cause: “This is because they defied Allah and His Messenger,” or “thalika bi-annahum shaqqoo Allaha wa rasoolahu,” the striking of the necks and the fingertips was because the disbelievers had “shaqqoo,” defied, opposed, split from, Allah and His Messenger, and the word “shaqqoo” conveys the breaking away, the standing in the opposition against the Lord and the one He had sent, and the commentators explain that the defiance of the Quraysh was the cause of the punishment that the previous verse described, for the one who defies the Lord and His Messenger has placed himself outside the protection of both. Ibn Kathir notes the precision of the word: the disbelievers had not merely erred but had actively split from the command, the “shiqaq” that the verse names, and the striking of the angels was the recompense of the split.\n\nThe second part is the principle: “And whoever defies Allah and His Messenger, then know that Allah is surely severe in punishment,” or “wa man yushaqiqi Allaha wa rasoolahu fa-inna Allaha shadeedu al-‘iqab,” the generalization of the cause into the law, the declaration that the defiance of the Lord and His Messenger invites, in every age and for every soul, the severity of the divine punishment, and the commentators note the function of the generalization: the verse does not limit the principle to the Quraysh of Badr but extends it to every defier, so that the listener understands that the fate of the defiers of Badr is the fate that awaits the defiers of every time. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the gravity of the defiance, for the opposition to the Lord and His Messenger is not a small matter but the sin that draws the severe punishment, and the believer is taught to guard against every form of the “shiqaq,” the splitting from the command, that the verse names. The verse is the warning against the defiance, and the statement of the punishment that it invites.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the cause and the law, and the commentators connect them to the believer’s own allegiance. The disbelievers of Badr were struck because they defied the Lord and His Messenger, and the believer is taught by the verse to stand on the opposite side, the side of the obedience that the defiance opposes, for the one who obeys the Lord and His Messenger is the one who is spared the severity that the defiance draws. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the explanation of the punishment of the previous verse, and that the believer reads in it the permanent principle: the defiance of the Lord and His Messenger is the cause of the punishment, and the obedience is the cause of the safety, and the choice between the two is the choice that the verse places before every soul. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the defiance: the cause stated, the principle generalized, and the severity declared — the warning that the defier of the Lord and His Messenger meets the punishment that his defiance has earned.",
  "14": "“That ˹worldly punishment˺ is yours, so taste it! Then the disbelievers will suffer the torment of the Fire” records the address to the disbelievers and the two punishments it declares, and the commentators note the sequence of the two. The verse turns from the description of the battle to the address of the defeated: “That worldly punishment is yours, so taste it!” or “thalikum fa-thooqoohu,” the first punishment, the “thalikum,” the worldly punishment of the battle, the slaughter and the defeat that the disbelievers suffered at Badr, and the command to taste it, the “thooqoo,” the tasting of the punishment that the hands of the believers and the angels had delivered, and the commentators note the force of the address: the verse speaks to the disbelievers in the second person, the direct address of the victor to the defeated, and the command to taste is the declaration that the worldly punishment was theirs, the first installment of the recompense. Ibn Kathir explains the meaning: the disbelievers of Badr tasted the punishment of the world in the defeat and the slaughter, and the verse declares it to them as the beginning of their account.\n\nThe verse then states the second punishment: “Then the disbelievers will suffer the torment of the Fire,” or “wa anna lil-kafireena ‘athaba an-nar,” the punishment of the Hereafter, the Fire that awaits the disbelievers after the punishment of the world, and the commentators note the word “anna,” then, the sequence of the two, for the worldly punishment is not the end but the beginning, and the Fire is the completion, the torment that follows the defeat as the Hereafter follows the world. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the completeness of the recompense: the disbeliever does not escape with the punishment of the world, for the world is only the first station, and the Fire is the second, and the two together are the full measure of the defiance that the previous verse named. The verse is the warning to the disbeliever and the assurance to the believer, the declaration that the worldly defeat is the foretaste of the eternal punishment, and that the taste of the first is the warning of the second.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the two punishments, and the commentators connect them to the believer’s perspective on the world. The believer who sees the disbeliever punished in the world knows that the worldly punishment is not the final word, and the verse teaches that the account of the disbeliever is not settled by the defeat of the world, for the Fire remains, and the believer is summoned to the gratitude of the one who has been spared both punishments, the defeat of the world and the torment of the next. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the address to the disbelievers of Badr, and that the believer reads in it the comfort of the justice: the punishment that the disbelievers tasted in the world is the proof that the justice of God is real, and the Fire that follows is the proof that the justice is complete. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the two punishments: the worldly defeat tasted, and the Fire declared — the first and the second installments of the recompense that the defiance earned.",
  "15": "“O believers! When you face the disbelievers in battle, never turn your backs to them” records the command of steadfastness in the battlefield, and the commentators note the prohibition and its occasion. The verse addresses the believers with the command of the battle: “When you face the disbelievers in battle, never turn your backs to them,” or “ya ayyuha allatheena amanoo itha laqeetumu allatheena kafaroo zahfan fa-la tuwalloohumu al-adbar.” The word “zahf,” the advancing, the meeting of the two forces in the march toward each other, is the scene, and the prohibition is the flight: “do not turn your backs,” the “tawalli al-adbar,” the retreat that shows the back to the enemy, and the commentators explain that the verse legislates the duty of the steadfastness, the prohibition of the flight from the battlefield, the fixedness of the foot that the faith demands. Ibn Kathir notes the occasion: the verse was revealed in the context of Badr, and it established the law of the battle for the believers of every age, the command that the one who meets the enemy in the field of the faith does not turn his back, for the flight is the betrayal of the cause and the brotherhood that the believer stands for.\n\nThe commentators dwell on the absolute form of the prohibition. The verse does not say “do not flee when you can fight” but “never turn your backs,” and the absoluteness is the teaching, for the believer in the field of the faith has no direction but the forward, and the turning of the back is the surrender of the very thing for which the battle is fought. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the Quran’s law of the battlefield, and that the steadfastness it commands is the outward form of the inner faith, for the foot that is planted in the field is the sign of the heart that is planted in the truth, and the turning of the back is the outward sign of the inward flight. The verse is the command of the courage that the faith demands, and the prohibition of the retreat that the fear suggests, and the believer is summoned to the first and warned against the second.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the exception that the next verse states, and the commentators connect the two. The absolute prohibition of the flight is qualified, as the next verse will show, by the permission of the manoeuvre and the joining of the troops, and the qualification teaches that the prohibition is against the cowardly flight, not against the tactical movement, for the faith is not the enemy of the strategy, and the believer who withdraws to rejoin his force is not the believer who turns his back in the panic. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the command of the steadfastness, and that the believer reads in it the standard of his own courage: the meeting of the enemy in the field of the faith is the test of the foot, and the foot that holds is the foot of the believer, while the foot that turns is the foot of the one whose faith has not reached the ground. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the steadfastness: the meeting of the forces, the prohibition of the flight, and the command of the foot — the law of the battlefield that the faith establishes.",
  "16": "“And whoever does so on such an occasion—unless it is a manoeuvre or to join their own troops—will earn the displeasure of Allah, and their home will be Hell. What an evil destination!” records the threat against the one who flees, and the commentators note the exception and the punishment. The verse states the threat against the flight: “And whoever does so on such an occasion,” or “wa man yuwallihim yawma’ithin duburahu,” the one who turns his back on the day of the battle, the “yawma’ithin,” the day of the meeting, and the exception that follows: “unless it is a manoeuvre or to join their own troops,” or “illa mutaharrifan li-qitalin aw mutahayyizan ila fi’ah,” the two permitted withdrawals, the “taharrif,” the turning aside for the tactical advantage, the manoeuvre of the soldier who shifts his position to strike better, and the “tahayyuz,” the withdrawing to join his own force, the regrouping of the one who falls back to the support of his company, and the commentators explain that the exception preserves the legitimacy of the strategy, for the prohibition is against the panic and the cowardice, not against the skill and the prudence of the soldier. Ibn Kathir notes the precision of the exception: the two movements that the verse permits are both for the sake of the fight, the manoeuvre and the regrouping, and the permission teaches that the flight that is forbidden is the flight that abandons the fight.\n\nThe verse then states the punishment: “will earn the displeasure of Allah, and their home will be Hell. What an evil destination!” or “fa-qad ba’a bi-ghadabin mina Allahi wa ma’wahu jahannamu wa bi’sa al-maseer.” The two punishments — the displeasure of God, the “ghadab,” the wrath that the flight earns, and the Hell that is its home, the “ma’wa” of the one who fled — and the closing exclamation, “what an evil destination,” the “bi’sa al-maseer” that the Quran reserves for the worst of the ends, and the commentators note the severity of the threat, for the flight from the battlefield is counted, in the law of the Quran, among the major sins, the abandonment of the brothers and the cause at the moment of their need. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the weight of the retreat: the one who turns his back on the enemy in the hour of the battle has earned the wrath of God and the Fire, and the severity of the threat is the measure of the duty, for the steadfastness in the field is not a small thing but a trust whose breach is the great sin that the verse names. The verse is the warning against the flight, and the statement of the end that the flight earns.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the exception and the rule, and the commentators connect them to the believer’s understanding of the courage. The permission of the manoeuvre and the regrouping teaches that the law of the battle is not the law of the fool, and the believer is commanded to fight with the intelligence that the faith sanctifies, the skill that serves the steadfastness and the prudence that serves the cause, while the flight that the verse threatens is the flight of the coward, the abandonment of the field that no strategy can excuse. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse completes the command of the previous verse, and that the believer reads in it the full law of the battlefield: the steadfastness commanded, the strategy permitted, and the flight threatened with the wrath and the Fire. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the retreat: the exception stated, the flight threatened, and the destination declared — the wrath of God and the Fire for the one who turns his back on the field of the faith.",
  "17": "“It was not you ˹believers˺ who killed them, but it was Allah Who did so. Nor was it you ˹O Prophet˺ who threw ˹a handful of sand at the disbelievers˺, but it was Allah Who did so, rendering the believers a great favour. Surely Allah is All-Hearing, All-Knowing” records the correction of the believers’ understanding of the victory, and the commentators explain the two negations and the event they refer to. The first negation: “It was not you believers who killed them, but it was Allah Who did so,” or “fa-lam taqtuloohum wa lakinna Allaha qatalahum,” the declaration that the killing of the disbelievers at Badr was not the work of the believers’ own strength but the work of God, and the commentators note the meaning: the verse does not deny that the believers struck the blows, but it corrects the attribution of the outcome, for the victory was not the fruit of the believers’ power but the gift of the divine aid, and the one who attributes the victory to himself has forgotten the Source of the victory. Ibn Kathir explains the context: the believers, after the victory, had boasted of their kills, each saying “I killed so-and-so,” and the verse descended to return the attribution to its true Owner, so that the pride of the victor might be replaced by the gratitude of the servant.\n\nThe second negation refers to the famous event of the pebbles: “Nor was it you O Prophet who threw a handful of sand at the disbelievers, but it was Allah Who did so,” or “wa ma ramayta ith ramayta wa lakinna Allaha rama.” The commentators relate the event: at a moment of the battle, the Prophet took a handful of pebbles and cast it toward the enemy, and the pebbles, by the divine power, reached the faces of the entire host, so that every one of the disbelievers felt the dust in his eyes, and the cast of a single handful became the blow that the whole army felt, and the verse declares that the throw was not the throw of the Prophet but the throw of God, for the handful that reached the thousand eyes was the work of the divine power, not of the human hand. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the complete theology of the victory: the killing and the throwing were both the acts of God, and the believers and the Prophet were but the instruments, the hands through which the divine power worked, and the recognition of this is the “great favour,” the “bala’ hasan,” that the verse names, the testing and the favour of the victory that the believer receives with the gratitude of the one who knows that the power was not his own.\n\nThe verse closes with the two names that seal the lesson: “Surely Allah is All-Hearing, All-Knowing,” or “inna Allaha samee‘un ‘aleem,” the Lord who heard the boasts of the victors and knew the pride that the victory might breed, and the commentators note the placement of the names, for the All-Hearing, All-Knowing is the witness of the hearts, and the correction of the attribution is the mercy of the One who heard the boasts and corrected them before the pride could take root. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the guard against the arrogance of the victory, and that the believer reads in it the permanent lesson: the triumph is the gift of God, and the one who says “I killed” and “I threw” has forgotten that the power was the Lord’s, and the gratitude that the favour demands is the acknowledgment of the true Source. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the attribution: the killing corrected, the throwing corrected, and the favour named — the victory returned to its Owner, and the believer taught that his strength is the strength of the Lord.",
  "18": "“As such, Allah frustrates the evil plans of the disbelievers” states the principle of the divine frustration of the plots, and the commentators note the brevity and the law that it declares. The verse is a single sentence, and its brevity is the point: “As such, Allah frustrates the evil plans of the disbelievers,” or “thalikum wa anna Allaha moohinu kaydi al-kafireen,” the declaration that just as God defeated the disbelievers at Badr, so He weakens and frustrates the “kayd,” the plots, the schemes, the stratagems of the disbelievers in every age, and the word “moohin” — the one who weakens, undermines, makes futile — is the divine name of the frustration, the attribute by which the Lord undoes the plans of His enemies. Ibn Kathir explains the meaning: the victory of Badr was not a single event but an instance of the permanent law, the “sunnah” of God by which the plots of the disbelievers are weakened and their schemes are turned against them, and the verse teaches that the believer’s confidence rests not upon the single victory but upon the law that the victory revealed. The plots of the Quraysh — the march to Badr, the confidence of the numbers, the plan to crush the believers — were all frustrated, and the frustration is the pattern of every plot against the truth.\n\nThe commentators dwell on the word “kayd,” the plot, and its meaning. The “kayd” is the secret scheming, the planning of the harm in the dark, the stratagem that the disbeliever devises against the believer, and the verse declares that God is the “moohin” of the “kayd,” the weakener of the plot, for the plot that is devised against the truth is a plot that the truth’s Lord undermines, and the underminer of the plot is the One who sees the plot before it is laid and turns it upon the one who laid it. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the assurance of the believer against the schemes of his enemies: the believer is not left to face the plots of the disbelievers alone, for the Lord who frustrates the plots is with him, and the frustration of the plots is the shield that no plot can pierce, for the one who schemes against the servant of God schemes against the Lord of the servant. The verse is the comfort of the believer, and the warning of the schemer.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the law of the frustration, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trials. The believer who is plotted against — in the family, in the market, in the society — is the beneficiary of the same law that frustrated the Quraysh, and the verse teaches that the plots of the enemies are the instruments of their own frustration, for the “kayd” that is laid against the truth is the “kayd” that the Lord weakens, and the believer who trusts the Lord watches the plot of his enemy become the means of his own deliverance. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the summary of the battle of Badr, and that the believer reads in it the permanent truth of the divine protection: the plots of the disbelievers are weakened by the Lord, and the weakening of the plots is the law that the victory of Badr revealed. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the frustration: the plots laid, the schemes devised, and the Lord the weakener of them all — the assurance that the plot against the truth is the plot that the truth’s Lord undoes.",
  "19": "“If you ˹Meccans˺ sought judgment, now it has come to you. And if you cease, it will be for your own good. But if you persist, We will persist. And your forces—no matter how numerous they might be—will not benefit you whatsoever. For Allah is certainly with the believers” records the address to the disbelievers after the victory, and the commentators note the four parts of the sentence. The first is the judgment: “If you Meccans sought judgment, now it has come to you,” or “in tastaftihoo fa-qad ja’akum al-fath,” the reference to the prayer of Abu Jahl, who had said before the battle, “O Allah, judge between us,” and the verse declares that the judgment that was sought had come, the “fath,” the decisive verdict of the battle, and the commentators note the irony: the disbelievers had called for the judgment, and the judgment came, and the judgment was against them, for the one who calls upon God to judge has called down the judgment that the truth of his cause deserves. Ibn Kathir relates the background: Abu Jahl had prayed on the eve of the battle for the judgment between the two camps, and the verse answers the prayer with its fulfillment, the defeat that the prayer had summoned.\n\nThe second and third parts are the alternatives: “And if you cease, it will be for your own good. But if you persist, We will persist,” or “wa in tantahoo fa-huwa khayrun lakum wa in ta‘oodoo na‘ud,” the offer of the cessation and the warning of the persistence, for the disbelievers were offered the return, the “intahaa,” the ceasing of the opposition, and the good that the cessation would bring them, and the verse couples the offer with the warning, the declaration that the persistence in the opposition would be met with the persistence of the divine punishment, the “na‘ud” that answers the “ta‘oodoo,” the persistence of the Lord against the persistence of the enemy. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the mercy of the warning: even after the defeat, the disbelievers were offered the return, and the door of the cessation was held open, and the persistence that the verse threatened was the choice that the disbelievers themselves would make, for the Lord does not persist against the one who ceases.\n\nThe fourth part is the declaration of the ultimate truth: “And your forces—no matter how numerous they might be—will not benefit you whatsoever. For Allah is certainly with the believers,” or “wa lan tughniya ‘ankum fi’atukum shay’an wa law kathurat wa anna Allaha ma‘a al-mu’mineen.” The numbers are declared useless, the “fi’ah,” the force, the host, the numerous, is no benefit against the one who is with God, and the verse closes with the declaration that God is with the believers, the “ma‘a al-mu’mineen” that is the source of every victory and the explanation of every defeat. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the final word of the account of Badr, and that the believer reads in it the complete truth of the battle: the judgment that was sought came, the cessation was offered, the persistence was warned, and the numbers were declared useless, for the only force that matters is the presence of the Lord with the believers. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the judgment: the verdict delivered, the return offered, the persistence warned, and the numbers annulled — the declaration that the Lord is with the believers, and that the force that stands with the truth is the force that no number can withstand.",
  "20": "“O believers! Obey Allah and His Messenger and do not turn away from him while you hear ˹his call˺” is the direct ethic for the spoils generation after Badr, the same ethic that would have kept the Sabbath town from being made apes and the gate-enterers from changing “Hittah.” The previous verse offered the Meccans judgment, cessation as good, persistence met with persistence, and forces no matter how numerous not benefiting, for Allah is with believers. Now the believers are addressed: “O believers! Obey Allah and His Messenger and do not turn away from him while you hear his call,” or “ya ayyuha allatheena amanoo atee‘oo Allaha wa rasoolahu wa la tawallaw anhu wa antum tasma‘oon.” “Atee‘oo,” obey, is the same obedience commanded in “if you are true believers obey Allah and His Messenger” regarding spoils and as earlier “follow what was sent down and do not take other guardians.” “Ta wallaw,” turn away, is the same turning that is forbidden in battle unless for manoeuvre, the same turning away from signs for arrogance that even if they see every sign they will not believe, and the same turning of those who say “We hear but do not listen.” The qualifier “while you hear his call,” or “wa antum tasma‘oon,” emphasizes that turning after hearing is more blameworthy than unawareness, as earlier “Your Lord would never destroy a society while its people are unaware” removed excuse, and the mountain covenant removed “We were not aware,” and the primordial testimony removed “We were unaware of this.” Hearing makes responsibility heavier.\n\nThe call, or “da‘wah,” is the same call that gives life, as the next verse will say “Respond to Allah and His Messenger when he calls you to that which gives you life, and know Allah stands between a person and their heart,” and the same call the warner gives as a clear warner, not an insane companion. Hearing it and obeying is the fertile land producing abundantly by the Lord’s leave; hearing and turning is the infertile hardly producing, the cattle-like who have ears they do not hear with, eyes they do not see with, hearts they do not understand with, entirely heedless.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats obedience as limited to the battlefield. Here obedience while hearing extends to the spoils’ distribution decided by Allah and His Messenger; turning away from that decision after hearing is the same as turning backs in battle, earning displeasure and Hell as home. For successors who firmly abide by Scripture and establish prayer where reward is never discounted, this is the daily listening that the previous “When the Quran is recited listen attentively and be silent so you may be shown mercy” enjoined, keeping the barrier’s hope of “Enter Paradise, no fear for you” within reach before the Hour that takes by surprise.",
  "21": "“Do not be like those who say, ‘We hear,’ but in fact they are not listening” follows the command to obey Allah and His Messenger and not turn away while you hear his call. The previous verse gave the positive command; this verse gives the negative example to avoid. The phrase “Do not be like those who say, ‘We hear,’ but in fact they are not listening” or “wa la takoonoo ka-allatheena qaloo sami‘na wa hum la yasma‘oon,” describes the hypocrites and the stubborn Meccans who verbally claimed hearing, as earlier the town by the sea tricked the Sabbath, the gate-enterers changed “Hittah,” and the chiefs said “We have heard, we could produce similar ancient fables.” Saying “We hear” is easy, but “la yasma‘oon,” not listening, means not giving the attentive silence that brings mercy when the Quran is recited, not using ears they were given, the same ears that many jinn and humans have but do not hear with, becoming like cattle, even less guided, entirely heedless.\n\nThe warning links to Badr’s context where the believers’ dispute about truth after it was clear was a whisper away from this trait. To say “We hear” about spoils belonging to Allah and His Messenger while persisting in dispute would be to join those who hear without listening, whose hearts do not tremble, whose faith does not increase when verses are recited, and who do not trust. Their hearing is like the idols who are called to guidance but cannot hear and face you but do not see.\n\nThe verse also echoes the earlier deaf and dumb who do not understand, the worst of beings in Allah’s sight, and those whose hearts and eyes are turned as they refused to believe at first, even if they see every sign they will not believe. Saying without listening is the first step to that sealing.\n\nA common misunderstanding equates physical hearing with listening. The Quran distinguishes: listening, or “sama‘,” with intent is the fertile soil that produces abundantly, silent and attentive so mercy is shown. Saying without listening is the infertile that hardly produces despite rain, like the successor who inherits Scripture well-versed yet seizes lowly gain. For the spoils community, the call is to be among those who, when the Quran is recited, listen attentively and are silent, who remember inwardly morning and evening with humility and are not among the heedless, so that elevated ranks, forgiveness, and honourable provision remain theirs.",
  "22": "“Indeed, the worst of all beings in the sight of Allah are the ˹wilfully˺ deaf and dumb, who do not understand” defines the rank of those who say “We hear” but do not listen. The previous verse warned not to be like them; this verse evaluates them. The phrase “Indeed, the worst of all beings in the sight of Allah,” or “inna sharra al-dawabbi inda Allahi,” uses “dawabbi,” moving creatures, beings that crawl, walk, the same term for cattle but broader, indicating the lowest in the sight of the Lord whose creation and command belong to Him alone, blessed Lord of all worlds. The worst are not the physically impaired but the wilfully “deaf and dumb, who do not understand,” or “al-summu al-bukmu allatheena la ya‘qiloon.” “Summ,” deaf, they have ears they do not hear with; “bukm,” dumb, they have tongues they do not speak truth with; “la ya‘qiloon,” they do not use “aql,” intellect to bind knowledge, the same intellect earlier said seldom you are mindful and will you not then understand and that perhaps you will become mindful.\n\nThis definition mirrors the anatomy of heedlessness given for many jinn and humans destined for Hell who have hearts not understanding, eyes not seeing, ears not hearing, like cattle, even less guided, entirely heedless, and the Sabbath town where fish tricks showed understanding had been abandoned. It also mirrors the idol audit: have feet to walk? hands to hold? eyes to see? ears to hear? The worst beings share the idols’ lack, while the best beings—those nearest to your Lord not too proud to worship, glorifying and prostrating, remembering morning and evening—share the angels’ faculties.\n\nThe verse links to Badr’s dynamic where faith increased by recitation distinguishes the heavy scales from the light, the gracious who enjoin right and turn from the ignorant from the iteratively plunged associates of devils sparing no effort.\n\nA common error pities the wilfully deaf as victims. The Quran judges them as worst, because they choose deafness after hearing the clear Book explained with knowledge as guide and mercy, after the mountain raised like a cloud, after the primordial “Am I not your Lord?” and after the sea split. For listeners dividing spoils, the warning is to not become worst by claiming to hear the Messenger’s distribution while harbouring turning, so that the beautiful provision of elevated ranks is not traded for the posture of the worst, whose home is the bed of Hell with flames as cover for the wicked.",
  "23": "“Had Allah known any goodness in them, He would have certainly made them hear. ˹But˺ even if He had made them hear, they would have surely turned away heedlessly” explains why the worst deaf and dumb remain so deaf despite being addressed by the Quran’s recitation. The previous verse judged them worst because they do not understand; this verse traces the divine knowledge behind that state. The opening, “Had Allah known any goodness in them, He would have certainly made them hear,” or “wa law alima Allahu feehim khairan la-asma‘ahum,” means if there were “khair,” good, remnant of willingness, fertile capacity, gratitude, the Lord who tests with prosperity and adversity so perhaps they would return, who varies lessons for the thankful, who guides with truth a community, would have made them hear with the hearing that increases faith, not merely with ears. The fact He did not make them hear shows He knew no good remained, as earlier “We did not find most of them true to covenant, rather most were rebellious,” and as “We will turn away from My signs those who act arrogantly without right, even if they see every sign they will not believe.”\n\nThe second clause adds a deeper layer: “But even if He had made them hear, they would have surely turned away heedlessly,” or “wa law asma‘ahum la-tawallaw wa hum mu‘ridoon.” This shows stubbornness is layered: not only would superficial hearing be withheld from the good-less, but even if hearing were granted, they would turn, or “tawallaw,” the same turning forbidden in battle unless for manoeuvre and forbidden while you hear his call, and they would be “mu‘ridoon,” actively averting, like those who turn away from ridicule only to return or who lie in ambush hindering from the Path and making it seem crooked.\n\nThe verse balances the earlier “If they see the Right Path they will not take it, but if they see a crooked path they will follow it” and the dog’s panting whether chased or left. It demonstrates that the flaw is not lack of volume but lack of inner khair that made drowsiness serenity and rain purification for the believers at Badr; the same rain made the enemy’s ground muddy and hearts terrified.\n\nA common misunderstanding uses this verse to claim determinism excuses them. The Quran shows the knowledge of no goodness follows persistent choices: claiming “We have heard” while not listening, taking faith as play, dividing shares unfairly, demanding signs to deceive. For the spoils community, the lesson is to keep goodness alive by being among those whose hearts tremble, who listen attentively and are silent so mercy is shown, whose prayer is established, who donate, whose ranks remain elevated, before the heart is known to have no good and hearing is withheld entirely.",
  "24": "“O believers! Respond to Allah and His Messenger when he calls you to that which gives you life. And know that Allah stands between a person and their heart, and that to Him you will all be gathered” is the positive call that corresponds to the negative warning not to be like those who say we hear but do not listen and not to be the worst deaf and dumb, and to the diagnosis that had Allah known any good He would have made them hear. The opening, “O believers! Respond to Allah and His Messenger when he calls you to that which gives you life,” or “ya ayyuha allatheena amanoo istajeeboo lillahi wa li-al-rasooli itha da‘akum li-ma yuhyeekum,” uses “istajeeboo,” answer, respond, the same response earlier praised as only the attentive will respond while the dead will be raised and the blind will remain. The call, or “da‘wah,” to what gives life, or “yuhyeekum,” is comprehensive: the Quran that is insight, guide, and mercy, the remembrance that makes fertile land produce, the humble secret calling with hope and fear that keeps mercy close, the just measure and the prayer, the “Hittah” at the gate, the holding firmly to Scripture, the staff that devoured illusion, the sea that saved and drowned, all give life to hearts dead from taking devils as masters thinking they are guided and from feeling secure from planning as losers do.\n\nThe warning that follows is intimate and sobering: “And know that Allah stands between a person and their heart,” or “wa i‘lamoo anna Allaha yahoolu baina al-mar’i wa qalbihi.” “Yahoolu,” stands between, intervenes, comes between a person and the control of his own heart, indicating how near His knowledge and will are, closer than the self, able to turn hearts as He turns winds and as He turned hearts and eyes of those who refused at first, able to open chests to Islam or make them tight as if climbing into the sky. It is the same nearness as “We were never absent” and “We have written for him guidance and mercy for those who stand in awe.” No one should delay response thinking the heart will remain available; the One between heart and person may seal it.\n\nThe final clause, “and that to Him you will all be gathered,” or “wa annahu ilaihi tuhsharoon,” returns to the gathering that frames all terms, the weighing just, the barrier, the heights, the Fire’s begging for water, the promise awaited whose fulfilment finds self-ruin. Gathering is the ultimate response, whether willing now or compelled then.\n\nA common error postpones response, assuming the heart will remain inclined later. This verse says the heart’s turning is not guaranteed to remain yours to command, and the appointed term for each community and each soul cannot be delayed, so the fertile call must be answered while the call itself is what gives life, before the heart is known to have no goodness and hearing is withdrawn.",
  "25": "“Beware of a trial that will not only affect the wrongdoers among you. And know that Allah is severe in punishment” follows the call to respond to what gives life and the warning that Allah stands between a person and their heart and that to Him you will all be gathered. The previous verse invited immediate response; this verse widens the consequence of not responding beyond individual deafness to communal contagion. The phrase “Beware of a trial that will not only affect the wrongdoers among you,” or “wa ittaqoo fitnatan la tuseebanna allatheena zalamoo minkum khassah,” uses “fitnah,” trial, affliction that tempts, tests, and confuses, like the fish on Sabbath, the calf’s lowing, the spoils dispute, the prosperity that makes some say “We will be forgiven” while seizing again. “La tuseebanna … khassah” not only the wrongdoers exclusively means the fitnah spreads, as earlier corruption after order spreads when a few lie in ambush on every road hindering the Way, or when a few change “Hittah,” or when a few defraud measure. A single society’s sin can bring night surprise while asleep or day while at play to all, as famine touched Pharaoh’s people generally and as the earthquake took the Sabbath breakers while warners were rescued but the silent middle group’s fate was left ominous.\n\nThe link to Badr’s context is direct: the spoils dispute among believers was a fitnah that, if not settled by mindfulness, settling affairs, and obedience, could affect more than the disputants, breaking the firm steps the rain had given and the serenity the drowsiness had given, turning the beautiful test of spoils into a trial that weakens the front that must never turn backs.\n\nThe warning, “And know that Allah is severe in punishment,” or “wa i‘lamoo anna Allaha shadeedu al-iqab,” is the same severity for defying Allah and His Messenger after which worldly taste and Fire’s torment follow, and the swift punishment but certainly forgiving, merciful pairing after the mountain raised and after dispersion. Severity here is communal: the fitnah that is not contained by the mindful becomes a severe overtaking like the dreadful punishment for the Sabbath’s rebelliousness or the wiping as if never there for Midian’s rejecters.\n\nA common misunderstanding isolates wrongdoing as private, harming only the doer. The verse teaches some trials are collective, like the collective term for a community that cannot be delayed, and like the hearts that, once turned, affect eyes and ears that do not hear. For those told to be gracious, enjoin right, and turn from the ignorant, the defence is to respond to what gives life promptly, to be among those who warn against evil so as to be rescued, and to remember that the earth belongs to Allah and outcome to the righteous who seek help and are patient, before the fitnah that spares no bystander arrives.",
  "26": "“Remember when you had been vastly outnumbered and oppressed in the land, constantly in fear of attacks by your enemy, then He sheltered you, strengthened you with His help, and provided you with good things so perhaps you would be thankful” brings the community back to its pre-Badr memory to heal the fitnah that spares no one. The previous verse warned of a trial affecting more than wrongdoers and that Allah is severe; this verse recalls the mercy that preceded the trial of spoils, grounding gratitude. The phrase “Remember when you had been vastly outnumbered and oppressed in the land, constantly in fear of attacks by your enemy,” or “wa uthkuroo ith antum qaleelun mustad‘afoona fil-ardi takhafoona an yatakhattafakum al-nas,” describes the Meccan period and early Medina when believers were few, “qaleel,” weak, “mustad‘af,” fearing being snatched, like the lowly who believed among Thamud questioned by the arrogant, the magicians before their prostration, and the Children of Israel before the sea. “Then He sheltered you,” or “fa-awakum,” gave refuge, Medina as shelter, like the Ark, the cave, the cloud shade, “strengthened you with His help,” or “wa ayyadakum bi-nasrihi,” the same help promised as a thousand angels murdifeen and as rain that purified and firmed steps and bound hearts, “and provided you with good things so perhaps you would be thankful,” or “wa razaqakum mina al-tayyibati la‘allakum tashkuroon,” tayyibat being the same good things earlier asked “Who has forbidden adornments and tayyibat Allah brought forth?” and the same provision seldom thanked after establishment.\n\nThe purpose, “so perhaps you would be thankful,” or “la‘allakum tashkuroon,” is the fertile response, the abundant production by the Lord’s leave, the thankful for whom lessons are varied, the mindful for whom the Home of the Hereafter is far better, the ones whose scales are heavy with ranks and forgiveness and honourable provision. It directly counters the rival response “This is what we deserve” in prosperity or blaming Moses in adversity that marked Pharaoh’s people and the successor who says “We will be forgiven” while seizing again.\n\nA common error after victory and spoils is to claim strength as self-made. The verse attributes shelter, strengthening help, and tayyibat entirely to Him, inviting the disputants to settle affairs with taqwa and obedience, to give from what He provided, and to count spoils as part of the tayyibat given to be eaten without waste but with thanks, before the collective fitnah that does not afflict only wrongdoers spreads from a heart between which Allah stands.",
  "27": "“O believers! Do not betray Allah and the Messenger, nor betray your trusts knowingly” addresses the spoils and fitnah directly with a name for the sin that turns a trial into a collective punishment. The previous verse reminded the believers they were few, oppressed, fearing snatch, then sheltered, strengthened, and provided with tayyibat so perhaps they would be thankful, a recount of help that should produce thankfulness, not dispute. Now the new command, “Do not betray Allah and the Messenger, nor betray your trusts knowingly,” or “ya ayyuha allatheena amanoo la takhoonoo Allaha wa al-rasoola wa takhoonoo amanatikum wa antum ta‘lamoon,” defines betrayal, or “khiyanah,” as the opposite of the amanah, trust, that includes the covenant not to say about Allah except truth, the holding firmly to the Scripture under the mountain, the primordial “Am I not your Lord?” and the specific trust of spoils decided by Allah and His Messenger, the deposit of judgment to the best of judges, and the just measure in the market. “Wa antum ta‘lamoon,” knowingly, stresses that betrayal here is not heedless like the Sabbath fish hidden but deliberate after hearing while you hear his call and after responding to what gives life where Allah stands between person and heart.\n\nThe link to spoils is precise: disputing the Messenger’s distribution of the surplus, hiding part of it, or claiming it by personal collection is betrayal of Allah and Messenger and of amanah, like the one who indulges in unlawful gains after inheriting Scripture and says “We will be forgiven,” seizing again when similar gain comes. The same khiyanah was shown when gate-enterers changed “Hittah” and when the Sabbath town set nets before Sabbath, betraying the trust of the limit.\n\nThe verse also connects to the broader law that whoever defies Allah and His Messenger is severe in punishment, tasting worldly defeat and Fire, and that Allah frustrates the evil plans of the disbelievers. Betrayal hands the community to that frustration.\n\nA common misunderstanding restricts betrayal to headline treason. The Quran shows betraying trusts knowingly includes manipulating spoils, measure, and word, all of which, if left, become the fitnah that will not only affect wrongdoers. The antidote is the next verse’s reminder that wealth and children are only a test and with Allah is a great reward, so the lowly gain seized now must not betray the great reward kept, and the standard granted to the mindful must be sought rather than betrayed.",
  "28": "“And know that your wealth and your children are only a test and that with Allah is a great reward” explains why betraying Allah, the Messenger, and trusts knowingly is so tempting, especially around spoils. The previous verse warned not to betray those trusts knowingly; this verse names the bait. The phrase “And know that your wealth and your children are only a test,” or “wa i‘lamoo annama amwalukum wa awladukum fitnatun,” uses “fitnah,” the same trial that will not only affect wrongdoers, the same test of prosperity and adversity so perhaps they would return, the same dispersion in groups tested so perhaps they will return, the same fish on Sabbath and the lowing calf after the sea. Wealth, or “amwal,” includes spoils, harvest, trade goods, and the lowly gain the Scripture heirs seize; children, or “awlad,” includes family that was prayed for as good offspring when the light burden grew heavy and then associated with false gods when granted. Both are declared “only a test,” not the ultimate Home, not the honourable provision from the Lord, but the fertile-infertile probe like rain that can produce every fruit or hardly anything depending on thankfulness.\n\nThe counterpart, “and that with Allah is a great reward,” or “wa anna Allaha indahu ajrun azeem,” is the same “ajr azeem” promised as rivers beneath where bitterness is removed, as the tenfold and more for the good, as the elevated ranks and forgiveness, the noble Word fulfilled for what they patiently endured, the blessed eastern and western inheritance for the oppressed successors. This great reward is the true alternative to the lowly near gain, or “arad hatha al-adna,” that successors seized.\n\nThe link to Badr is direct: spoils are part of “amwal,” the disputed surplus; the children of the few oppressed who feared being snatched are part of “awlad” now present in the camp. To betray trusts knowingly for spoils is to choose the test object over the Tester, the near over the great.\n\nA common error treats wealth and children as proof of favour or as private possessions exempt from weighing. The verse places them on the scale that will be heavy or light, on the barrier where each is recognized by marks, and between the person and their heart where Allah stands. Knowing this helps the mindful call upon Him humbly and secretly with hope and fear, remain gracious, enjoin right, turn from the ignorant, seek refuge when Satan tempts, remember and see clearly, and thereby be among those who firmly abide by Scripture and establish prayer where reward is never discounted, rather than among those turned away from signs for arrogance, even if they see every sign they will not believe.",
  "29": "“O believers! If you are mindful of Allah, He will grant you a standard ˹to distinguish between right and wrong˺, absolve you of your sins, and forgive you. And Allah is the Lord of infinite bounty” promises the three gifts that answer the test of wealth and children and the pull to betray trusts. The previous verse said wealth and children are only a fitnah and with Allah is great reward; this verse shows how the great reward begins now for the mindful. The condition, “If you are mindful of Allah,” or “in tattaqoo Allaha,” is the same taqwa that is the best clothing, the fertile soil’s thankfulness, the morning and evening remembrance without heedlessness, the holding firmly to the Scripture under the mountain, the guarding that makes the Scale heavy. The reward, “He will grant you a standard to distinguish between right and wrong,” or “yaj‘al lakum furqana,” uses “furqan,” a criterion, separator, light that separates as the staff separated truth from great magic, as the sea separated oppressed successors from Pharaoh, as the barrier separates Paradise and Hell, as the weighing separates heavy from light. Furqan is the insight that lets the remembering mindful see clearly when Satan whispers, the same “basirah” from your Lord as guide and mercy for those who believe.\n\nThe next two gifts, “absolve you of your sins, and forgive you,” or “wa yukaffir ankum sayyi’atikum wa yaghfir lakum,” repeats the du‘a of the calf-remorseful, the magicians’ showered perseverance, and the seventy’s hasanah request, now as promised effect of furqan: sins are covered, or “takfeer,” and forgiven, or “maghfirah,” the same covering that removed bitterness from hearts with rivers beneath.\n\nThe seal, “And Allah is the Lord of infinite bounty,” or “wa Allahu thoo al-fadli al-azeem,” uses “fadl azeem,” tremendous grace, the same infinite bounty that gave a thousand angels murdifeen, that made twelve springs gush, shaded with clouds, sent manna and quails, and promised elevated ranks and honourable provision, surpassing any spoils.\n\nA common misunderstanding seeks furqan without taqwa, expecting criterion while persisting in betrayal. The verse ties it to mindfulness, showing that the dispute over spoils is itself the moment to become mindful: settle affairs, obey Messenger, respond to what gives life while Allah stands between person and heart, beware fitnah, remember shelter when few. Then the standard to judge between rightful distribution and lowly seizure becomes clear, and the great reward with Allah becomes the chosen gain, far better than the arad hatha al-adna that drew the Scripture heirs to say “We will be forgiven” while seizing again.",
  "30": "“And ˹remember, O Prophet,˺ when the disbelievers conspired to capture, kill, or exile you. They planned, but Allah also planned. And Allah is the best of planners” recalls the pre-Badr conspiracy that makes Badr’s help and furqan intelligible as answers to planning. The verse recalls the plot: “when the disbelievers conspired to capture, kill, or exile you,” or “wa ith yamkuru bika allatheena kafaroo li-yuthbitooka aw yaqtulooka aw yukhrijooka,” and the commentators identify the occasion as the famous gathering at Dar al-Nadwa, the council house of the Quraysh, where the chiefs of Mecca assembled to decide the fate of the Prophet, and where the three proposals — the imprisonment, the killing, and the exile — were debated until the plan of the assassination was agreed upon. Ibn Kathir relates the fuller account: Iblis himself came to the council in the guise of an old man of Najd, and he argued against the proposals of the imprisonment and the exile, pressing instead for the killing by a single stroke from the swords of all the tribes together, so that the blood of the Prophet would be spread among the clans and the clan of his kin could not demand the vengeance for it, and the plot was the “makr” that the verse names. The three options that the verse lists — the capture, the killing, and the exile — are the three proposals of the council, and the verse recalls them so that the believer may know what was plotted against the messenger of God.\n\nThe verse then states the answer to the plot: “They planned, but Allah also planned. And Allah is the best of planners,” or “wa yamkuroona wa yamkuru Allahu wallahu khayru al-makireen.” The two plans are set against each other — the plan of the disbelievers and the plan of God — and the commentators explain the meaning of the divine planning with the care that the phrase demands, for the “makr” of God is not the deceit of the schemer but the counter-arrangement of the truth, the divine response by which the plot of the enemy is turned against him, and the plan of God at the council of Dar al-Nadwa was the deliverance of the Prophet through the very night of the plot, the escape to the cave and then to Medina, and the frustration of the scheme by the One who sees the scheme before it is laid. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the ultimate truth of every plot: the enemies of the truth plan, and their planning is real, but the Lord of the truth plans, and His planning is the answer that the plot cannot anticipate, for the best of planners is the One who weaves the plot of the enemy into the thread of His own purpose. The verse is the assurance of the believer against the conspiracies of his enemies, and the declaration that the plot against the truth is the plot that the truth’s Lord turns.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the deliverance, and the commentators connect it to the Hijrah and to the battle of Badr that the surah describes. The plot of the council was the prelude to the Hijrah, for the escape from Mecca was the divine answer to the scheme, and the Hijrah led to Medina and to the battle of Badr, where the same disbelievers who had plotted the killing met the defeat that the surah recounts, so that the plan of the council and the victory of Badr are the two ends of the single thread of the divine planning, the plot and the counter-plot, the scheme and its frustration. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the summary of the divine protection of the Prophet, and that the believer reads in it the permanent truth of his own safety: the enemies may plot, but the Lord plans, and the best of planners is the guardian of the one whom He protects. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the plot and the plan: the council gathered, the schemes proposed, and the planning of the Lord the answer to them all — the assurance that the plot against the servant is the plot that the Lord has already planned against.",
  "31": "“Whenever Our revelations are recited to them, they challenge ˹you˺, ‘We have already heard ˹the recitation˺. We have already heard. If we wanted, we could have easily produced something similar. This ˹Quran˺ is nothing but ancient fables!’” records the disbelievers’ standard dismissal of the Quran that the previous verse’s conspiracy tried to silence. After recalling the capture-kill-exile plot and the best planning that turned it into hijra and Badr, this verse shows the intellectual arrogance that accompanied that hostility. The phrase “Whenever Our revelations are recited to them,” or “wa itha tutla alaihim ayatuna,” is the same recitation that should increase faith in those whose hearts tremble and that should be heard attentively and silently so mercy is shown. Instead their reply is, “We have already heard,” or “qad sami‘na,” we have heard, the same “We hear” claimed by those who do not listen, and then the boast “If we wanted, we could have easily produced something similar,” or “law nasha’u la-qulna mithla hatha.” This boast claims the Quran explained with knowledge, whose Word is perfected in truth and justice and whose most beautiful names are to be called upon, is within their capacity, the same claim later made as “I can reveal the like” in the worst wrong of fabricating lies, and the same dismissal as “This Quran is nothing but ancient fables,” or “in hatha illa asateeru al-awwaleen,” ancient tales, the same fables label used earlier for jinn association and for calling the well-known she-camel and twelve springs old stories.\n\nThe link to planning is direct: they planned to capture, kill, or exile the reciter, and when recitation persisted through his followers, they planned to neutralize it by calling it fables anyone could produce. Both are forms of “We will be forgiven” while seizing lowly gain, changing “Hittah” at the gate, and breaking the Sabbath with fish tricks, all ways of making the straight seem crooked.\n\nThe verse mirrors the earlier “There are some who pretend to listen but veils are cast and they say this is ancient fables,” the wilfully deaf and dumb worst beings, and those who, even if they see every sign, will not believe and will follow the crooked. Hearing without listening is the infertile soil that hardly produces.\n\nA common misunderstanding entertains the boast as plausible. The Quran’s challenge elsewhere to produce a surah like it remains unmet, the mountain raised like a cloud was not needed for a fable, and the barrier’s heights recognized truth by appearance. The boast is the arrogance for which gates do not open, whose deeds become vain, whose example is the dog panting whether chased or left. For the mindful who firmly abide and establish prayer, the Quran is not fables but the insight, guide, and mercy that, when listened to attentively in silence, brings the promised forgiveness, furqan, and infinite bounty.",
  "32": "“And ˹remember˺ when they prayed, ‘O Allah! If this is indeed the truth from You, then rain down stones upon us from the sky or overcome us with a painful punishment’” records the defiant prayer that follows the fables dismissal. The previous verse showed they said we have heard, we could produce similar, it is nothing but ancient fables. Now, instead of asking for guidance after hearing the Quran as insight, guide, and mercy, they pray for punishment, a mirror of the hastening challenge “Bring us what you threaten if you are truthful,” asked by ’Ad after being told to remember favours and by Thamud after killing the she-camel. The phrase “If this is indeed the truth from You, then rain down stones upon us from the sky,” or “in kana hatha huwa al-haqqa min indika fa-amtir alaina hijaratan mina al-sama’i,” invokes the very stones that elsewhere are the rajfah earthquake, the rain of brimstone on Lot’s people, and the dreadful punishment for Sabbath violators, now requested voluntarily, as if the Hour that is too tremendous for heavens and earth and that only takes by surprise were a spectacle to call down.\n\nThe alternative, “or overcome us with a painful punishment,” or “i’tina bi-athabin aleem,” repeats the painful, “aleem,” torment said to overtake those who harm the she-camel and that the wicked taste. Praying for stones rather than for hasanah in this world and the next, which Moses requested for his people after the earthquake, shows the hardening that makes even if they see every sign they will not believe and that makes hearts sealed so they will not hear.\n\nThe prayer’s form “O Allah! If this is indeed the truth…” acknowledges the possibility of truth yet prefers destruction to submission, the extreme of taking faith as play, of desiring large numbers and arrogance, of hindering on every road, and of being among the worst deaf and dumb who say we hear but do not listen.\n\nThe next verse will answer why stones did not immediately rain: Allah would never punish them while you O Prophet are in their midst, nor while they pray for forgiveness, showing that even defiant prayer for punishment is met with delay, the gradual drawing in ways they cannot comprehend, the flawless planning that gives rope until the appointed term that cannot be delayed, while the best planner shelters, strengthens, and provides tayyibat so perhaps they will be thankful, and grants furqan to the mindful. The lesson for those dividing spoils is to replace the stone-prayer with the h opening prayer for forgiveness and mercy lest they be losers.",
  "33": "“But Allah would never punish them while you ˹O Prophet˺ were in their midst. Nor would He ever punish them if they prayed for forgiveness” gives the merciful reason why stones did not rain after the defiant stone-prayer. The previous verse quoted them praying “If this is indeed the truth, rain stones upon us or bring painful punishment,” a challenge that should, by the logic of every community has a term that cannot be delayed once it comes, have been answered with immediate seizure. This verse explains the delay. The first protection, “while you O Prophet were in their midst,” or “wa ma kana Allahu li-yu‘aththibahum wa anta feehim,” identifies the Prophet’s presence as a mercy, as earlier the Book was sent down so you may warn and you may not let anxiety tighten your chest, and as the shelter that strengthened the few oppressed and the thousand angels were bushra. The Prophet is the same warner who is only to convey, the unlettered Prophet whose light relieves burdens, whose following is to be rightly guided. As long as he is among them, the punishment that took societies at night while asleep is held back, like the mountain held over them as a cloud, giving time for the reminder “Am I not your Lord?” to be remembered.\n\nThe second protection, “Nor would He ever punish them if they prayed for forgiveness,” or “wa ma kana Allahu mu‘aththibahum wa hum yastaghfiroon,” is even broader, extending beyond the Prophet’s physical presence to any’s seeking forgiveness, or “istighfar,” the same forgiveness the calf-remorseful sought crying “If our Lord does not have mercy and forgive we will be losers,” and that Moses sought for himself and his brother as the most merciful of the merciful, and that is ordained as mercy’s vastness for those who shun evil. Seeking forgiveness is the humble secret calling with hope and fear that keeps mercy close to good-doers and that turns the tight chest spacious.\n\nTogether the two protections explain the “I only delay them for a while, but My planning is flawless” and “We will gradually draw them in ways they cannot comprehend,” not abandonment but flawless delay where furqan can be granted to the mindful and spoils can be settled with taqwa.\n\nA common misunderstanding reads the stone-prayer as proof of courage. The verse shows the courage that matters is to pray for forgiveness, not for stones, and that the community’s continued existence itself is a sign that punishment was withheld for the Prophet’s sake and for the possibility of istighfar, inviting the Meccan and Medinan listeners to replace hastening challenges with the hasanah prayer for good in this world and the next, before He shows the home of the rebellious and before the Hour that knowledge is only with the Lord arrives by surprise.",
  "34": "“And why should Allah not punish them while they hinder pilgrims from the Sacred Mosque, claiming to be its rightful guardians? None has the right to guardianship except those mindful ˹of Allah˺, but most pagans do not know” explains why, despite the temporary protection of the Prophet’s presence and the possibility of forgiveness, punishment remains deserved and near. The previous verse said Allah would never punish while the Prophet is among them nor while they seek forgiveness, giving delay. This verse asks “And why should Allah not punish them,” or “wa ma lahum alla yu‘aththibahumu Allahu,” while they do two things that warrant the severe that defiers taste and the dreadful that overtook Sabbath breakers. First, “while they hinder pilgrims from the Sacred Mosque,” or “wa hum yasuddoona an al-masjidi al-haram,” the same hindering on the Way by lying in ambush on every road and making it seem crooked, here applied to the House whose prayer was whistling and clapping, whose guardianship they claim. Second, “claiming to be its rightful guardians,” or “wa ma kanoo awliya’ahu,” while none is its guardian except the mindful, or “awliya,” the same wilayah that belongs to Allah who revealed the Book and protects the righteous, and that was warned not to take others as guardians besides Him.\n\nThe divine correction, “None has the right to guardianship except those mindful of Allah,” or “in awliya’uhu illa al-muttaqoon,” uses “muttaqoon,” the best clothing, the standard to distinguish, the fertile thankful, the ones whose hearts tremble, who firmly abide by Scripture and establish prayer where reward is never discounted. Guardianship of the Sacred House is not by lineage to Qusayy or by large numbers and arrogance that are of no use, but by taqwa, as the Heights recognized the humble by marks. The closing, “but most pagans do not know,” or “wa lakinna aktharahum la ya‘lamoon,” repeats that most do not know, as most did not know that all omens are with Allah, that the Quran is ancient fables is ignorance, and that even if they see every sign they will not believe because they are heedless.\n\nThis verse links Badr’s station as defender of the Sacred Mosque’s true guardianship: the battle that was promised to establish truth and uproot disbelievers was also the vindication of the House’s rightful walis. A common error thinks ancestry gives sanctuary rights regardless of hindering the pilgrim. The Quran says hindering the Way while claiming wilayah is precisely why punishment, though delayed for the Prophet’s presence, is still deserved, and why the spoils’ distribution by Allah and His Messenger, not by the hinders, settles who truly is mindful before the best of planners who frustrated their evil plans.",
  "35": "“Their prayer at the Sacred House was nothing but whistling and clapping. So taste the punishment for your disbelief” exposes the emptiness behind the claim to guardianship of the Sacred Mosque. The previous verse asked why Allah should not punish those who hinder pilgrims while claiming to be its awliya, none of whom are except the mindful, but most do not know. This verse describes what they did in that House they claimed to guard. The phrase “Their prayer at the Sacred House was nothing but whistling and clapping,” or “wa ma kana salatuhum inda al-baiti illa mukaan wa tasdiyah,” uses “mukaan,” whistling, and “tasdiyah,” clapping, sounds that mock worship, a play that makes faith as amusement and play for which the Fire’s water is forbidden and today We forget them as they forgot the meeting. Their salat by the House, whose rightful salat is to establish prayer and to enter bowing with “Hittah” and to remember inwardly morning and evening with humility, was transformed into entertainment that neither speaks to them nor guides, like the calf that lowing neither spoke nor guided and the idols that have no feet, hands, eyes, ears and cannot answer or help.\n\nThis whistling-clapping worship is the same corruption after order as the Sabbath fish trick and the gate-enterers’ word change, and the same elegant deception that inclines hearts of those who disbelieve in the Hereafter to be pleased and persistent. It is the worship of the heedless, worst of beings deaf and dumb who do not understand, who have hearts not understanding, eyes not seeing, ears not hearing, even less guided than cattle.\n\nThe sentence that follows is the recompense for that hollow guardianship and hollow prayer: “So taste the punishment for your disbelief,” or “fa-thooqoo al-athaba bima kuntum takfuroon,” the same taste commanded for defiers who were struck at necks and fingertips, for those whose prayer was whistling to taste, and for the Fire’s residents who taste boiling drink while water is forbidden. Taste links the worldly taste at Badr to the Fire’s taste later, showing severity in this world and Fire beyond for those who defy.\n\nA common misunderstanding reduces punishment to afterlife only. The verse shows the taste begins now for the custodians who hindered the Way, whose evil plans Allah frustrates and whose forces no matter how numerous will not benefit because Allah is with the believers whose hearts tremble, faith increases, trust is in the Lord, prayer is established, and donation is from what He provided.",
  "36": "“Surely the disbelievers spend their wealth to hinder others from the Path of Allah. They will continue to spend to the point of regret. Then they will be defeated and the disbelievers will be driven into Hell” traces the economic strategy behind hindering pilgrims from the Sacred Mosque and whistling-clapping worship. After tasting punishment for disbelief, this verse shows how disbelief finances itself. The phrase “Surely the disbelievers spend their wealth to hinder others from the Path of Allah,” or “inna allatheena kafaroo yunfiqoona amwalahum li-yasuddoo an sabeeli Allah,” names spending, or “yunfiqoon,” the same verb used for believers who donate from what We have provided, but inverted. Believers’ infaq establishes prayer and gives zakat for hasanah in both worlds; disbelievers’ infaq lies in ambush on every road, finances caravans to arm against Badr, pays magicians to deceive eyes, and later funds the Sacred House’s whistling worship while blocking pilgrims. The same amwal that are a test, fitnah, with children, become the instrument of sadd, hindering.\n\nThe next sentence predicts the financial and military outcome: “They will continue to spend to the point of regret. Then they will be defeated and the disbelievers will be driven into Hell,” or “fa-sa-yunfiqoonaha thumma takoonu alaihim hasratan thumma yughlaboon wa allatheena kafaroo ila Jahannama yuhsharoon.” Spending becomes “hasrah,” anguish, regret, like the hasrah of those who said “Woe to us for having ignored this” with burdens on backs, and the hasrah of those who took faith as play and were then ignored. “Yughlaboon,” defeated, is the same defeat of Pharaoh and his people put to shame when truth prevailed and illusions failed. “Yuhsharoon,” driven, gathered, is the same gathering to the True Master, to the Fire where bed and cover await the arrogant and gates do not open.\n\nThe spending that was meant to hinder becomes itself the pile that the next verse says will be gathered to separate evil from good: He will pile evil upon evil and cast it into Hell, the true losers.\n\nA common error views spending as neutral. The Quran shows spending to hinder is the fitnah that will not only affect wrongdoers, requiring the mindful to respond to what gives life while Allah stands between person and heart. For the spoils holders, the contrast is stark: believers spent to be sheltered and strengthened with help and given tayyibat, now given furqan and infinite bounty; disbelievers spend to hinder and will reap hasrah, defeat, and driving into Hell, the complete frustration of their kaid whose planning is mateen for Allah alone, whose victory is only from Almighty, All-Wise.",
  "37": "“so Allah may separate the evil from the good. He will pile up the evil ones all together and then cast them into Hell. They are the ˹true˺ losers” gives the divine purpose behind the spending to hinder, the regret, defeat, and driving into Hell. The previous verse showed disbelievers spending to hinder from the Path, spending to regret, then defeated and driven. This verse says that sequence is not accidental but to separate, or “li-yameeza Allahu al-khabeetha mina al-tayyib,” that Allah may distinguish the foul, impure, from the pure, good, the same distinction as fertile land producing abundantly by its Lord’s leave versus infertile hardly producing, the same varying of lessons for the thankful versus hardly for others, the same separating of evil from good promised so the wicked may become distinct. “Khabeeth,” foul, is the bad land, the lowing calf, the whistling-clapping prayer, the deceiving eyes’ great magic, the fish trick on Sabbath, the lowly gain seized while claiming “We will be forgiven.” “Tayyib,” good, is the tayyibat provided, adornment brought forth, the honourable provision from the Lord for true believers, elevated ranks and forgiveness.\n\nThe next phrase, “He will pile up the evil ones all together and then cast them into Hell,” or “wa yaj‘ala al-khabeetha ba‘dahu ala ba‘din fa-yarkumahu jamee‘an fa-yaj‘alahu fi Jahannam,” depicts accumulation, like the lowly gain piled, like evil plans piled, like groups entering Hell cursing predecessors, like wrongdoers made allies of one another, like devils’ associates extended in ghayy without curtailment. “Yarkumahu,” to pile, heap, is the harvest of the infertile, heaped for burning, unlike the twelve springs divided justly for each tribe.\n\nThe verdict, “They are the true losers,” or “ula’ika humu al-khasiroon,” uses the same loser name given to those threatened for following Shu‘ayb, those who followed Shu‘ayb being true losers reversed, the losers who feel secure from Allah’s planning, the self-ruined whose gods fail them, the scales light who doom themselves.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats defeat as random. The verse shows defeat is sorting: evil heaped and cast, good lifted. For those dividing spoils, this sorting is the ultimate accounting after the beautiful test, where Allah frustrates kaid and is with believers whose hearts tremble, whose faith increases, who trust, establish, and donate, so that the evil piled from hindering and spending is cast, and the good from obeying while you hear remains as lasting provision.",
  "38": "“Tell the disbelievers that if they desist, their past will be forgiven. But if they persist, then they have an example in those destroyed before them” opens the door that the previous sorting into evil heaped and true losers seemed to close, extending to the disbelievers the same mercy offered after calf worship. The previous verse piled evil to cast into Hell, the true losers. This verse says the pile is not yet final for the living. The address, “Tell the disbelievers that if they desist, their past will be forgiven,” or “qul lillatheena kafaroo in yantahoo yughfar lahum ma qad salafa,” uses “yantahoo,” to desist, to end hindering, whistling, spending to block, defying, like ceasing the ambush on every road and the mockery of fables and the stone-prayer. “Yughfar lahum ma qad salafa,” what has passed will be forgiven, is the same forgiveness prayed for by Adam’s children after the tree, by the magicians after belief without permission, by the calf-remorseful upon seeing they strayed, and by Moses for himself and his brother as most merciful of the merciful, and the vast mercy that encompasses everything and is ordained for those who shun evil, pay zakat, and believe, now offered to the very hinders whose evil would otherwise be heaped.\n\nThe alternative, “But if they persist, then they have an example in those destroyed before them,” or “wa in ya‘oodoo fa-qad madat sunnatu al-awwaleen,” means if they return to spending, hindering, defying, then the established way, or “sunnah,” of the ancients has already passed: Noah’s flood for the blind, ’Ad uprooted, Thamud lifeless after she-camel, Lot’s brimstone rain, Midian as if never there, Pharaoh drowned in the sea for denying signs with heedlessness. That sunnah is the appointed term that cannot be delayed, the surprise at night while asleep or day while at play, the dreadful punishment for the Sabbath’s rebelliousness and the disgrace of apes, the punishment from heaven for changing “Hittah.”\n\nThis verse mirrors the Badr offer, “If you cease it is for your good, if you persist We will persist, your forces no matter how numerous will not benefit, for Allah is with believers.” Both show judgment has come but forgiveness remains before persistence seals.\n\nA common error despairs that past hindering cannot be forgiven while claiming “We will be forgiven” without desisting. The verse corrects both: forgiveness is real if they desist, but not without desisting, the same condition “those who commit evil then repent and become true believers, your Lord is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.” The spoils community, reminded not to betray trusts knowingly and that wealth and children are fitnah, is thus invited to be the desist-ers whose past is forgiven, rather than the persist-ers whose example is already written as He destroyed what Pharaoh constructed and made the oppressed successors.",
  "39": "“Fight against them until there is no more persecution—and ˹your˺ devotion will be entirely to Allah. But if they desist, then surely Allah is All-Seeing of what they do” sets the objective of fighting after offering forgiveness if they desist and warning of the ancients’ example if they persist. The previous verse said if disbelievers desist past is forgiven, if they persist the sunnah of the ancients has passed; this verse defines the fight’s limit. The phrase “Fight against them until there is no more persecution—and your devotion will be entirely to Allah,” or “wa qaatiloohum hatta la takoona fitnatun wa yakoona al-deenu kulluhu lillah,” uses “fitnah,” the same trial that will not only affect wrongdoers and that is wealth and children, here meaning shirk-associated persecution, like hindering pilgrims from the Sacred Mosque claiming guardianship, lying in ambush, making the Way seem crooked, whistling-clapping worship, spending to hinder, and defying Allah and His Messenger. Fighting until no fitnah means until the systematic ability to persecute and to make the straight seem crooked is removed, so that devotion, or “deen,” the Way itself, becomes wholly for Allah, the same deen that the Heights hoped to enter and the righteous establish with justice, the deen that the unlettered Prophet commands good and forbids evil for.\n\nThis purpose clarifies Badr’s purpose to establish truth by His Words and uproot disbelievers, to make truth prevail and batil fail even to the dismay of the wicked. It is not fighting for spoils, for the spoils belong to Allah and His Messenger, nor for numbers, for numbers do not benefit, but for fitnah’s removal so that the Book explained with knowledge can be a guide and mercy without hindrance.\n\nThe conditional after, “But if they desist, then surely Allah is All-Seeing of what they do,” or “fa-in intahaw fa-inna Allaha bima ya‘malona baseer,” mirrors the cessation that is for your good and that ensures past forgiveness, but adds that Allah is Baseer, All-Seeing, who was never absent, who sees the raised mountain, the split sea, the lowing calf, the changed gate words, the Sabbath fish, and the heart between which He stands. Desisting is seen; persisting is seen; devotion becoming entirely for Allah is seen.\n\nA common misunderstanding frames this as compulsion in religion. The Quran elsewhere states no compulsion in faith; fitnah here refers to the coercive system that hinders the Way, not private belief, as earlier the call was to no compulsion but to make the crooked straight so the mindful may choose. Fighting until devotion is entirely for Allah means until the environment allows sincere devotion, the humble secret calling with hope and fear, the gracious enjoining of right and turning from the ignorant, and the firm abiding by Scripture without ambush.",
  "40": "“And if they do not comply, then know that Allah is your Protector. What an excellent Protector, and what an excellent Helper!” gives the final assurance if the disbelievers do not desist from persecution and devotion becoming entirely for Allah. The previous verse said fight until no persecution and devotion is entirely for Allah, but if they desist Allah is All-Seeing; this verse says if they do not comply, “then know that Allah is your Protector,” or “wa in tawallaw fa-i‘lamoo anna Allaha mawlakum.” “Tawallaw,” turn away, is the same turning warned as not to turn away while you hear and not to turn backs in battle except for manoeuvre. If they turn away from desisting, the believers’ protection is not numbers or spoils but the Protector, or “Mawla,” the same Wali who protects the righteous who revealed the Book, whose earth belongs to Allah and whose outcome belongs to the righteous, with whom Allah is with the believers, whose help is a thousand angels murdifeen, whose rain purifies and firms steps, whose angels say “I am with you, make believers firm, I will cast horror.”\n\nThe exclamation that follows, “What an excellent Protector, and what an excellent Helper!” or “ni‘ma al-mawla wa ni‘ma al-naseer,” is the same “ni‘ma” used for the best of planners, the best of judges, the most merciful, the most beautiful names to call upon. “Mawla” is guardian allied to those who do not take other guardians; “Naseer” is helper who gives victory that comes only from Almighty, All-Wise. This pair mirrors the earlier “Allah is your Protector, what an excellent Protector and what an excellent Helper” that will come after spoils are detailed, showing that whether the context is persecution ending or spoils dividing, the answer is the same Mawla-Naseer.\n\nThe verse links the spoils’ opening “Their distribution is decided by Allah and His Messenger” to its close: if you obey, spoils are settled by taqwa; if enemies persist, your help is not spoils but the Protector. It also answers the wealth-and-children fitnah and the betrayal of trusts knowingly: trusting the Protector and Helper outweighs betraying for lowly gain.\n\nA common error after Badr is to rely on the spoils or the thousand angels as independent guarantees. This verse corrects that reliance is on the Mawla whose planning is flawless and whose promise to give the upper hand over either target was fulfilled by establishing truth and uprooting disbelievers, inviting the mindful to respond to what gives life, to not be like those who say “We hear” but do not listen, to avoid being worst deaf and dumb, and to know Allah stands between person and heart so desisting or persisting is seen, but protection and help remain excellent for those whose hearts tremble, faith increases, trust remains, prayer is established, and donation is from what He provided.",
  "41": "“Know that whatever spoils you take, one-fifth is for Allah and the Messenger, his close relatives, orphans, the poor, and ˹needy˺ travellers, if you ˹truly˺ believe in Allah and what We revealed to Our servant on that decisive day when the two armies met ˹at Badr˺. And Allah is Most Capable of everything” gives the detailed law that was deliberately delayed when the first question about spoils was answered. When the companions first asked about “al-anfal,” the surplus, the answer was not the division but the heart’s correction: be mindful of Allah, settle your affairs, and obey if you are true believers whose hearts tremble and whose trust is in the Lord. Now, after Badr’s lessons of crying for help, angelic bushra, rain that purified and firmed steps, and the truth prevailing over illusion, the distribution is clarified with precision, showing that law follows purification.\n\nThe opening “Know that whatever spoils you take” addresses any gain acquired as a group through striving where the creation and command belong to Allah, whose earth belongs to Him to grant to whom He chooses. “One-fifth is for Allah and the Messenger, his close relatives, orphans, the poor, and needy travellers” defines the “khums,” the fifth. The share for Allah and His Messenger is not a personal treasure but a public trust administered by the Messenger for the community’s needs, beginning with his close relatives, or “thi al-qurba,” who were forbidden regular charity, then orphans, the poor, and the wayfarer, the same needy traveller earlier sheltered when the believers were few and oppressed and were provided tayyibat so perhaps they would be thankful. This priority shows spoils are not a wage for the brave alone but a means to uplift the vulnerable and keep kinship ties, the same relatives earlier taught not to be betrayed in trust.\n\nThe condition “if you truly believe in Allah and what We revealed to Our servant on that decisive day when the two armies met at Badr” ties distribution to belief, specifically belief in the revelation that made the heavy clouds rain on lifeless land producing fruit and the angels’ promise, the same decisive Day, or “yawm al-furqan,” the Day of Criterion when truth and falsehood were separated as at the sea’s parting and the staff’s devouring. Belief in that Day’s revelation proves the spoils are not “This is what we deserve” prosperity illusions.\n\nThe seal “And Allah is Most Capable of everything” or “Allahu ala kulli shay’in qadeer” recalls the blessed Lord who created heavens and earth in six Days and whose command subjects sun, moon, and stars and whose angels and rain made the few appear many and then few again as He willed. A common misunderstanding treats the fifth as a tax on fighters. The verse shows it is a right of those who could not fight but whose needs keep the community just, ensuring the trail from shelter when few to honourable provision remains thankfully shared.",
  "42": "“˹Remember˺ when you were on the near side of the valley, your enemy on the far side, and the caravan was below you. Even if the two armies had made an appointment ˹to meet˺, both would have certainly missed it. Still it transpired so Allah may establish what He had destined—that those who were to perish and those who were to survive might do so after the truth had been made clear to both. Surely Allah is All-Hearing, All-Knowing” reconstructs Badr’s geography to reveal Lordship over appointment itself. The verse describes the terrain: “when you were on the near side of the valley, your enemy on the far side, and the caravan was below you,” or “ith antum bil-‘udwati ad-dunya wa hum bil-‘udwati al-quswa war-rakbu asfala minkum,” and the commentators explain the geography of Badr: the Muslims were on the near side of the valley, the side closer to Medina, the Quraysh on the far side, the side closer to Mecca, and the caravan of Abu Sufyan was below, along the coastal route, having slipped past the Muslims and out of their reach, so that the three parties — the Muslims, the army, and the caravan — were arrayed across the terrain in the positions that the verse records. Ibn Kathir notes the detail of the geography, for the verse uses it to make a larger point about the divine arrangement of the meeting.\n\nThe verse then states that point: “Even if the two armies had made an appointment to meet, both would have certainly missed it,” or “wa law tawa‘adtum lakhtalaftum fi al-mee‘adi,” the declaration that the meeting of the two armies was not the result of any human arrangement, for the two forces had not planned to meet at Badr, and had they tried to arrange the meeting, the arrangement would have failed, so that the meeting was the work of the divine decree alone, the “mee‘ad” that God Himself had set. Al-Sa’di observes the significance of the sentence: the verse teaches that the battle of Badr was not an accident of the marching but an appointment of the Lord, the convergence of the two armies at the wells of Badr by a decree that no human planning could have produced, and the recognition of the appointment is the recognition of the One who appointed it. The verse then states the purpose of the arrangement: “Still it transpired so Allah may establish what He had destined—that those who were to perish and those who were to survive might do so after the truth had been made clear to both,” or “wa lakin li-yaqdiya Allahu amran kana maf‘oolan li-yahlika man halaka ‘an bayyinatin wa yahya man hayya ‘an bayyinah,” the declaration that the battle was fought so that the truth might be made clear to both sides, so that the one who perished would perish upon the clear proof and the one who survived would survive upon the clear proof, and the commentators dwell on this, for the verse teaches that the divine judgment is always preceded by the clarity, and the destruction and the salvation at Badr were both grounded upon the truth that had been made manifest. Ibn Kathir notes the closing names: “Surely Allah is All-Hearing, All-Knowing,” the witness of the clarity and the judge of the two camps.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the divine appointment, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s trust. The believer who knows that the meetings of his life — the trials, the confrontations, the battles — are the appointments of the Lord, not the accidents of the chance, faces them with the peace of the one whose steps are ordered, and the verse teaches that the convergence of the opposing forces is the work of the decree that the believer trusts. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the geography of Badr turned into the theology of the appointment, and that the believer reads in it the comfort of the ordered life: the near side and the far side, the caravan below, and the meeting that no planning could have arranged — all the work of the One who appoints, and all for the clarity that the judgment requires. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the appointment: the terrain described, the meeting decreed, and the purpose declared — the clarity of the truth for the perishing and the surviving alike, under the hearing and the knowledge of the Lord.",
  "43": "“˹Remember, O Prophet,˺ when Allah showed them in your dream as few in number. Had He shown them to you as many, you ˹believers˺ would have certainly faltered and disputed in the matter. But Allah spared you ˹from that˺. Surely He knows best what is ˹hidden˺ in the heart” records the dream of the Prophet and the divine management of the morale, and the commentators explain the dream and its purpose. The verse recalls the dream: “when Allah showed them in your dream as few in number,” or “ith yureekahumu Allahu fee manamika qaleelan,” the vision in which God showed the Prophet the enemy as few, and the commentators note the occasion: on the eve of Badr, the Prophet saw in his dream the army of the Quraysh as a small force, and the dream was the first of the divine reassurances, the vision that prepared the Prophet to face the host without the fear that the true number would have provoked. Ibn Kathir explains the purpose of the dream: “Had He shown them to you as many, you believers would have certainly faltered and disputed in the matter,” or “wa law arakahum katheeran la-fashiltum wa la-tanaza‘tum fi al-amri,” for the sight of the full army would have broken the resolve of the believers and set them to disputing about the wisdom of the battle, and the dream that showed them few spared the believers the faltering and the dispute, so that the dream was the mercy that the battle’s courage required.\n\nThe verse then states the divine knowledge that governed the arrangement: “But Allah spared you from that. Surely He knows best what is hidden in the heart,” or “wa lakinna Allaha sallama innahu ‘aleemun bi-thati as-sudoor.” The sparing was the work of the One who knows the hearts, the “thati as-sudoor,” the contents of the chests, the hidden fears and the hidden resolves, and the commentators note the connection: the dream was proportioned to the knowledge of the hearts, for the Lord who knew the weakness of the believers showed them what their weakness could bear, and the management of the vision was the management of the One who sees the hidden. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the divine care in the details of the trial: the Lord does not burden the heart beyond its strength, and the trials are proportioned to the capacity, and the dream of the few was the mercy that the knowledge of the hearts had prescribed. The verse is the record of the divine management, and the assurance that the believer is not tested with what he cannot bear.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the stages of the reassurance, and the commentators connect it to the next verse and to the battle itself. The dream showed the enemy few before the battle, and the next verse records that on the field the enemy was made to appear few in the eyes of the believers and the believers few in the eyes of the enemy, and the two visions are the two stages of the single reassurance, the dream for the eve and the perception for the day, and both are the work of the One who manages the hearts. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the first of the descriptions of the divine management of the morale, and that the believer reads in it the source of his own calm: the Lord who showed the enemy few to the Prophet is the Lord who proportions the trials of the believer to the strength of the heart, and the heart that trusts the Lord is the heart that the Lord steadies. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the dream: the enemy shown few, the faltering spared, and the knowledge of the hearts declared — the divine management of the courage that the battle required.",
  "44": "“Then when your armies met, Allah made them appear as few in your eyes, and made you appear as few in theirs, so Allah may establish what He had destined. And to Allah ˹all˺ matters will be returned ˹for judgment˺” records the second stage of the divine management, and the commentators explain the two perceptions and their purpose. The verse describes the meeting: “when your armies met, Allah made them appear as few in your eyes, and made you appear as few in theirs,” or “wa ith yureekumoohum ith iltaqaytum fee a‘yunikum qaleelan wa yuqallilukum fee a‘yunihim,” the two-fold management of the sight, the enemy made to appear few in the eyes of the believers so that their courage might hold, and the believers made to appear few in the eyes of the enemy so that the enemy might advance in overconfidence and, as the commentators add, so that the full number of the believers might not terrify the enemy into the caution that would have strengthened them. Ibn Kathir explains the wisdom of the two perceptions: the believers saw the enemy as few and fought without despair, and the enemy saw the believers as few and advanced without preparation, and when the battle was joined, the enemy discovered the truth too late, for the believers appeared to them multiplied and the terror that the verse elsewhere records descended upon them, and the two perceptions were the two edges of the single divine management.\n\nThe verse then states the purpose: “so Allah may establish what He had destined,” or “li-yaqdiya Allahu amran kana maf‘oolan,” the fulfillment of the decree, the establishment of the divine purpose that the battle was appointed to accomplish, and the closing declaration: “And to Allah all matters will be returned for judgment,” or “wa ila Allahi turja‘u al-umoor,” the return of all affairs to God, the final judgment to which the battle of Badr and every human affair is heading, and the commentators note the placement of the closing, for the verse that began with the management of the sight ends with the return of the affairs, so that the believer is taught to see the battle and its perceptions as the penultimate matters, the instruments of the decree that the final return will complete. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the complete truth of the divine management: the sight is managed, the decree is established, and the affairs return to the One who manages them, and the believer who trusts the Lord trusts the management, the decree, and the return alike.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the two sights, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own perception. The believer is taught that the eye is not the measure of the reality, and that the Lord who manages the sight manages the trial, for the enemy that appears overwhelming may be the enemy that the Lord has already diminished, and the believer who appears weak may be the believer that the Lord has already strengthened, and the truth of the matter is with the One who sees all and returns all to Himself. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse completes the description of the divine management of Badr, and that the believer reads in it the comfort of the ordered sight: the few and the many are the arrangements of the Lord, and the outcome of the battle is the decree that the arrangements serve. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the two perceptions: the enemy diminished, the believers diminished, and the decree established — the management of the sight for the fulfillment of the purpose, and the return of all to the One who manages.",
  "45": "“O believers! When you face an enemy, stand firm and remember Allah often so you may triumph” records the command of the steadfastness and the remembrance, and the commentators note the two commands and the result. The verse addresses the believers with the command of the battle: “When you face an enemy, stand firm,” or “itha laqeetum fi’atan fathbutoo,” the command of the “thubut,” the steadfastness, the fixedness of the foot and the heart in the face of the enemy, and the second command: “and remember Allah often,” or “wathkuroo Allaha katheeran,” the abundant remembrance of God in the hour of the battle, the “dhikr” that the verse commands as the companion of the steadfastness, and the result of the two: “so you may triumph,” or “la‘allakum tuflihoon,” the “falah,” the triumph, the success that the steadfastness and the remembrance together produce. Ibn Kathir explains the pairing: the steadfastness is the work of the body, the fixedness of the foot upon the field, and the remembrance is the work of the heart, the filling of the heart with the Lord in the hour of the danger, and the two together are the complete equipment of the believer, for the foot that is fixed without the heart that remembers is the foot of the mercenary, and the heart that remembers without the foot that holds is the heart of the coward, and the triumph is the fruit of the two joined.\n\nThe commentators dwell on the command of the remembrance in the battle, and its meaning. The battle is the hour of the greatest fear, and the remembrance of God is the remedy of the fear, for the heart that is filled with the Lord has no room for the terror of the enemy, and the “dhikr katheer,” the abundant remembrance, is the continuous turning of the heart to the Lord in the midst of the clash, the prayer that the believer carries into the fight. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the secret of the steadfastness: the foot holds because the heart remembers, and the triumph is granted because the remembrance is abundant, for the Lord who is remembered is the Lord who is present, and the presence of the Lord in the heart is the source of the courage that the field demands. The verse is the command of the battle, and the prescription of the triumph.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the two commands, and the commentators connect them to the believer’s own struggles. The enemy of the believer is not only the army of the field but the enemy of the soul, the desire and the fear and the temptation that the believer faces in the daily battle, and the command of the verse applies to both, for the believer who faces the inner enemy is commanded to stand firm and to remember his Lord often, and the triumph of the inner battle is the same triumph that the verse promises, the “falah” of the one who holds and remembers. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the command of the battle and the life, and that the believer reads in it the permanent law of the victory: the steadfastness and the remembrance are the two keys of the triumph, and the one who holds them holds the triumph in every field. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the two keys: the foot fixed, the heart remembering, and the triumph promised — the command of the battle and the prescription of the success.",
  "46": "“Obey Allah and His Messenger and do not dispute with one another, or you would be discouraged and weakened. Persevere! Surely Allah is with those who persevere” records the command of the unity and the perseverance, and the commentators note the three commands and their logic. The first command: “Obey Allah and His Messenger,” or “wa atee‘oo Allaha wa rasoolahu,” the obedience that is the root of the unity, for the obedience to the single command of the Lord and His Messenger is the bond that unites the believers, and the second command: “and do not dispute with one another,” or “wa la tanaza‘oo,” the prohibition of the dispute, the “niza‘,” the internal conflict that the previous verses had shown at the eve of Badr, and the commentators explain the consequence that the verse attaches to the dispute: “or you would be discouraged and weakened,” or “fa-tafshaloo wa tathhaba reehukum,” the “fashal,” the faltering, the discouragement, and the going of the “reeh,” the wind, the strength, the spirit, and the verse teaches that the dispute is the disease of the community, for the people who dispute with one another lose their courage and their strength, and the loss of the “reeh” is the loss of the very spirit that the victory requires. Ibn Kathir explains the image: the “reeh” is the wind, and the strength of the community is like the wind that fills the sails, and the dispute is the leak that lets the wind escape, and the community that disputes is the community that is emptied of its strength.\n\nThe third command is the perseverance: “Persevere! Surely Allah is with those who persevere,” or “wasbiroo inna Allaha ma‘a as-sabireen,” the command of the “sabr,” the patience, the steadfast endurance, and the promise that accompanies it, the declaration that God is with the patient, the “ma‘a as-sabireen” that is the source of every victory and the comfort of every trial. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the three pillars of the community’s strength: the obedience that unites, the avoidance of the dispute that preserves the unity, and the perseverance that endures the trial, and the three together are the complete counsel of the battle, for the community that obeys and does not dispute and perseveres is the community that the Lord is with, and the community that the Lord is with is the community that no enemy can defeat. The verse is the command of the unity, and the promise of the presence.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the sequence, and the commentators connect the three commands to the failure of Uhud and the victory of Badr. The believers had disputed at the eve of Badr, and the dispute had been settled by the divine arrangement, and the believers would dispute at Uhud, and the dispute would cost them the victory, and the verse is the warning that the sequence teaches: the obedience and the unity and the perseverance are the conditions of the divine presence, and the presence of the Lord is the condition of the victory. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the counsel of the battle and the community, and that the believer reads in it the permanent truth of the victory: the dispute is the disease, the perseverance is the remedy, and the presence of the Lord is the reward. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the unity: the obedience commanded, the dispute forbidden, and the perseverance promised — the three pillars of the strength, and the presence of the Lord with those who hold them.",
  "47": "“Do not be like those ˹pagans˺ who left their homes arrogantly, only to be seen by people and to hinder others from Allah’s Path. And Allah is Fully Aware of what they do” records the warning against the arrogance of the pagans, and the commentators note the two motives that the verse names. The verse warns the believers against the example of the disbelievers: “Do not be like those pagans who left their homes arrogantly, only to be seen by people and to hinder others from Allah’s Path,” or “wa la takoonoo kallatheena kharajoo min diyarihim bataran wa ri’a’a an-nasi wa yasuddoona ‘an sabeeli Allah,” and the commentators identify the occasion: the Quraysh had marched out of Mecca to the battle with the arrogance of the confident and the display of the proud, and the verse names the two motives of their march — the “batar,” the arrogance, the swelling of the pride, and the “ri’a’,” the showing off, the desire to be seen by the people, and the third, the hindering of others from the path of God, the “sadd,” the blocking of the way that the disbelievers had made their aim. Ibn Kathir explains the contrast that the verse draws: the believers were commanded to march in the obedience and the humility, and the disbelievers had marched in the arrogance and the display, and the verse warns the believers against the example of the disbelievers, for the march of the faith is the march of the humble, and the march of the display is the march of the falsehood.\n\nThe verse then states the divine awareness that judges the two marches: “And Allah is Fully Aware of what they do,” or “wallahu bima ya‘maloona muheet,” the encompassing knowledge of the Lord, the “muheet,” the One who surrounds the deeds, and the commentators note the function of the closing: the verse exposes the hidden motives of the disbelievers and declares that the Lord is fully aware of them, for the display that the people saw was the surface, and the knowledge of the Lord saw the arrogance and the hindering beneath the surface, and the awareness of the Lord is the judgment of the motives that the eye of the people could not see. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the sincerity of the march: the believer marches for the Lord, not for the display, and the believer knows that the Lord sees the motive of the march, so that the “ri’a’,” the showing off, is the corruption of the deed, and the one who marches to be seen has received his reward in the seeing, while the one who marches for the Lord has his reward with the One who is fully aware. The verse is the warning against the display, and the summons to the sincerity.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the two marches, and the commentators connect it to the outcome of the battle. The Quraysh marched with the arrogance and the display, and they met the defeat that the surah records, and the believers marched with the obedience and the trust, and they met the victory, and the two marches and the two outcomes are the teaching of the verse, for the march that is for the Lord is the march that the Lord crowns, and the march that is for the display is the march that the Lord frustrates. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the warning of the sincerity, and that the believer reads in it the permanent truth of the deed: the intention is the measure of the act, and the one who marches for the Lord is the one whom the Lord is aware of with the awareness of the reward. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the two marches: the arrogance named, the display exposed, and the awareness declared — the warning against the march of the show, and the summons to the march of the Lord.",
  "48": "“And ˹remember˺ when Satan made their ˹evil˺ deeds appealing to them, and said, ‘No one can overcome you today. I am surely by your side.’ But when the two forces faced off, he cowered and said, ‘I have absolutely nothing to do with you. I certainly see what you do not see. I truly fear Allah, for Allah is severe in punishment’” records the betrayal of Satan of his allies, and the commentators describe the scene and the lesson. The verse recalls the deception: “when Satan made their evil deeds appealing to them,” or “wa ith zayyana lahumu ash-shaytanu a‘malahum,” the beautifying of the disbelievers’ deeds by Satan, the “tazyin” by which the march of the Quraysh was made to appear glorious to them, and the promise that followed: “and said, ‘No one can overcome you today. I am surely by your side,’” or “wa qala la ghaliba lakumu al-yawma mina an-nasi wa inni jarun lakum,” the assurance of the victory and the pledge of the protection, the “jar,” the neighbour and the ally, and the commentators identify the scene: Satan, in the guise of a man of the tribe, marched with the Quraysh to Badr and assured them that their numbers and their strength were unbeatable, and that he himself would stand with them. Ibn Kathir relates the fuller account: Satan appeared in the form of Suraqah ibn Malik, the man of the Kinanah, and marched with the disbelievers, promising them the victory and the protection, and his presence was the morale that the falsehood had summoned.\n\nThe verse then records the betrayal: “But when the two forces faced off, he cowered and said, ‘I have absolutely nothing to do with you,’” or “fa-lamma tara’ati al-fi’atani nakasa ‘ala ‘aqibayhi wa qala innee baree’un minkum,” the retreat of Satan upon the sight of the angels, the “nakasa,” the turning upon his heels, and the declaration of the innocence, the “bara’ah,” the disowning of the allies he had promised to protect, and the reason that he gave: “I certainly see what you do not see. I truly fear Allah, for Allah is severe in punishment,” or “innee ara ma la tarawna innee akhafu Allaha wallahu shadeedu al-‘iqab,” the sight of the angels that the disbelievers could not see, and the fear of the punishment that the sight provoked, and the commentators note the irony of the confession: the one who had promised the protection was the first to flee, and the fear of Allah that he confessed was the very fear that the falsehood had denied, and the ally of the disbelievers abandoned them at the moment of the truth. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse is the Quran’s great lesson on the ally of the falsehood: Satan beautifies the deed and promises the protection, and then flees and disowns, and the one who trusts the promise of the falsehood is left, in the hour of the truth, with nothing but the abandonment, for the ally of the lie is the ally of the flight. The verse is the warning against the trust in the falsehood, and the assurance that the one who trusts the Lord is the one whose ally never flees.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the two allies, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The disbelievers trusted Satan, and Satan fled, and the believers trusted the Lord, and the Lord remained, and the two trusts and the two outcomes are the teaching of the verse, for the ally who is a creature is the ally who fails, and the ally who is the Creator is the ally who never abandons the one who trusts Him. Ibn Kathir notes that the verse is the conclusion of the account of Badr, and that the believer reads in it the permanent truth of the ally: the promise of Satan is the promise of the flight, and the promise of the Lord is the promise of the presence, and the believer chooses between the two in every trial. For the listener, the verse is the lesson of the fleeing ally: the deed beautified, the protection promised, and the flight and the disowning at the moment of the truth — the warning against the trust in the falsehood, and the summons to the trust in the Lord who never flees.",
  "49": "“˹Remember˺ when the hypocrites and those with sickness in their hearts said, ‘These ˹believers˺ are deluded by their faith.’ But whoever puts their trust in Allah, surely Allah is Almighty, All-Wise” records the whisper that accompanied the valley and the Satanic desertion, now from a human source inside. The previous verse showed Satan promising “No one can overcome you, I am by your side” and then fleeing, seeing what they do not. This verse adds the human echo: “when the hypocrites and those with sickness in their hearts said, ‘These believers are deluded by their faith,’” or “ith yaqoolu al-munafiqoona wa allatheena fi quloobihim maradun gharra ha’ula’i deenuhum.” The speakers are not the open pagans but the hidden “munafiqoon,” those who said “We hear but do not listen,” and those whose hearts are diseased, tight as if climbing into the sky, the same sickness earlier described for the hypocrites whom the light test exposes. Their claim “gharra,” deluded, deceived, is the same verb for worldly life deceiving those who took faith as play, implying the believers’ reliance on promises like “I will reinforce you with a thousand angels” and “Victory is only from Allah” is self-deception.\n\nThe Quran answers with the principle that has been the backbone of every story from Noah’s trust to Shu‘ayb’s “In Allah we trust” to the magicians’ “Surely to our Lord we will return”: “But whoever puts their trust in Allah, surely Allah is Almighty, All-Wise,” or “wa man yatawakkal ala Allahi fa-inna Allaha Azeezun Hakeem.” The same pairing closed the previous bushra verse, “Victory is only from Allah, Surely He is Almighty, All-Wise,” showing trust connects to the source of victory. “Azeez,” Almighty, Prevailing, like the mountain that crumbled and the sea that drowned and the earthquake that left lifeless, ensures trust is not delusion; “Hakeem,” All-Wise, ensures it is not reckless, as He knows what is hidden in hearts and differentiated the fifth, the dream few, and the double fewness with wisdom.\n\nThis slogan, “Are these deluded?” mirrors the disbelieving chiefs’ “We see you as fool, liar, misguided,” the same elite seeing. The answer mirrors the Heights’ “Are these the ones you swore would never receive mercy? Enter Paradise.” For the few who were once oppressed and feared snatching, then sheltered and strengthened with help and tayyibat so perhaps they would be thankful, trust is the fertile response that makes the same wind, the same rain, the same angels produce triumph. A common misunderstanding confuses trust with passivity. In Badr it meant standing firm, remembering often, obeying, not disputing, persevering while Allah is with those who persevere, the opposite of turning backs that earns wrath and Hell.",
  "50": "“If only you could see when the angels take the souls of the disbelievers, beating their faces and backs, ˹saying,˺ ‘Taste the torment of burning!’” shifts the lens from the battlefield’s visible horror in hearts to the unseen taking of souls that follows it, after the hypocrites’ “deluded by their faith” taunt was answered with trust in the Almighty, All-Wise. The phrase “when the angels take the souls of the disbelievers, beating their faces and backs” or “wa law tara ith yatawaffa allatheena kafaroo al-mala’ikatu yadriboona wujoohahum wa adbarahum” depicts the extraction at death as the same angels earlier sent as bushra and to make believers firm now striking, the same “yatawafaa” used for the soul-takers who never neglect and who will ask where false gods are, and whose faces and backs correspond to the two sides turned towards and away in battle. The beating is the worldly foretaste of “Taste the torment of burning” or “thooqo athaba al-hareeq,” the same “thooqoo” said for the whistling prayer’s punishment and for the defiers struck at necks and fingertips, now made the eternal burning taste.\n\nThe conditional “If only you could see” or “wa law tara” is the same “If only you could see when they are detained before the Fire” and “before their Lord” and “when souls are taken,” an invitation to witness with the heart what the eye will see when the barrier stands and the heights recognize. It is reserved for the Prophet and the mindful, not for the heedless who are like cattle.\n\nThis image links Badr’s two punishments, worldly defeat and Fire, earlier joined as “That is yours so taste it, and for the disbelievers is the Fire.” The angels who reassured the believers’ hearts now reassure justice: the same creation that gave drowsiness and rain to purify believers gives striking to the defiant who split from Allah and His Messenger and whose fate is that of Pharaoh’s people seized for sins.\n\nA common error separates battlefield outcomes from soul outcomes as unrelated. The verse joins them: the faces struck were the faces turned in arrogance to be seen, the backs struck were the backs that would turn in flight or that turned away while hearing his call, the same backs warned not to turn. For the community dividing spoils, the image warns against betraying trusts knowingly for wealth that is test, lest the honourable provision from the Lord be traded for a beating that begins at extraction and continues as burning.",
  "51": "“This is ˹the reward˺ for what your hands have done. And Allah is never unjust to ˹His˺ creation” explains the beating on faces and backs and the taste of burning as earned recompense, not arbitrary harshness. The previous verse showed angels taking souls with striking and commanding to taste burning; this verse says “This is the reward for what your hands have done” or “thalika bima qaddamat aydiyakum.” “Qaddamat aydiyakum,” what your hands sent forth, is the same earning language used for every ranking according to deeds, for the vain deeds of those who deny signs and the meeting, for the self-wronging that does not wrong Us but wrongs selves who changed words at the gate and tricked the Sabbath fish. The hands that divided crops unfairly for associates, carved calves that lowed, defrauded measure, lay in ambush on every road, and spent to hinder from the Path are the same hands now beaten.\n\nThe second clause “And Allah is never unjust to His creation” or “wa anna Allaha laisa bi-zallamin lil-abeed” uses “zallam,” intensive unjust, to deny any injustice to “abeed,” servants, the same servants like you that the idols are, the same servants who were given a Book explained with knowledge and promised that none will be wronged, that We never discount the reward of those acting righteously, and that whatever you spend in Allah’s cause will be paid in full and you will not be wronged. Upright, or “qist,” weight, just measure, not coming near orphan wealth except to enhance, entering the gate humbly with “Hittah,” all illustrate the justice that never wrongs.\n\nThis principle connects the battlefield’s “Allah frustrates the evil plans” and “Victory is only from Allah, Almighty, All-Wise” to the weighing that will be just where heavy scales succeed and light scales doom themselves. The beating taste is precisely what hands put forward, as earlier “They will receive what is destined for them until angels arrive asking where false gods you invoked are” and “Enter the Fire with groups of jinn and humans that preceded you, each cursing its predecessor.”\n\nA common misunderstanding imagines punishment as divine invention unrelated to deeds. The verse says it is the hands’ own sending forth, making the punishment the natural harvest of fertile versus infertile response to the same rain, the same valley, the same dream few. For the few sheltered and strengthened with help to perhaps be thankful, this is a warning not to send hands forth to betray Allah, Messenger, and trusts knowingly for spoils, lest the honourable provision that requires Elevated ranks, forgiveness, and the best of the Tablets be replaced by a beating that no intercessor can plead from when fulfilment is sought too late and gods fabricated fail.",
  "52": "“Their fate is that of the people of Pharaoh and those before them—they all disbelieved in Allah’s signs, so Allah seized them for their sins. Indeed, Allah is All-Powerful, severe in punishment” places Badr’s disbelievers in the long succession of seized peoples that has threaded this surah from Noah to Pharaoh. The phrase “Their fate is that of the people of Pharaoh and those before them—they all disbelieved in Allah’s signs” uses “da’b,” wont, habit, fate like the wont of those before, as earlier the wont of Pharaoh who demanded a sign of skilled magic, whose chiefs called Moses a driver from the land, whose people were afflicted with famine and shortage, floods, locusts, lice, frogs, blood, and then drowned for denying Our signs and being heedless, as earlier ’Ad was uprooted, Thamud struck by rajfah, Lot’s people poured with brimstone, Midian wiped as if never there. The common element is “they all disbelieved in Allah’s signs” or “kafaroo bi-ayatillah,” the same disbelief that denies Our signs and the meeting, that even if they see every sign will not believe, that turns away from signs for arrogance, that makes deeds vain.\n\nThe consequence “so Allah seized them for their sins” or “fa-akhathahum Allahu bi-thunoobihim” repeats the seizing, or “akhdh,” that took societies at night while asleep or day while at play, that seized the Sabbath breakers as apes, that seized the gate changers with punishment from heaven. “Bi-thunoobihim,” for their sins, shows the seizing is measured, like ranks according to deeds and like the piling of evil to cast into Hell, not random cruelty.\n\nThe names “Indeed, Allah is All-Powerful, severe in punishment” or “inna Allaha qawiyyun shadeedu al-iqab” combine the strength that made the mountain as a cloud and levelled it, that split the sea, that made the staff devour, with the severity already threatened for defying Allah and His Messenger where necks and fingertips are struck and faces and backs beaten. “Qawiyyun” All-Powerful parallels “Moohinu kaidi al-kafireen” and “Frustrates evil plans,” showing Allah’s power is the source of the spoils’ fifth and of the valley’s appointment.\n\nA common error treats Badr’s Quraysh as an exception due to kinship to the House. The verse says their wont is Pharaoh’s wont, so their seizure follows the same sunnah that has already passed, inviting them to desist so past is forgiven or to persist and taste the ancestors’ example, and inviting believers dividing spoils to remember that the same power that seized the arrogant also sheltered the few and strengthened them, so perhaps they will be thankful rather than dispute.",
  "53": "“This is because Allah would never discontinue His favour to a people until they discontinue their faith. Surely Allah is All-Hearing, All-Knowing” gives the theological law behind the seizing for sins and the favour to the sheltered, immediately after saying Allah seized Pharaoh’s wont for disbelieving signs as the All-Powerful, severe in punishment. The first clause “This is because Allah would never discontinue His favour to a people until they discontinue their faith” or “thalika bi-anna Allaha lam yaku mughayyiran ni‘matan an‘amaha ala qawmin hatta yughayyiroo ma bi-anfusihim” uses “mughayyiran,” to change, discontinue, alter a favour, “ni‘matan,” a favour, like establishment on earth with livelihood though seldom thanks, like shelter after being few and oppressed, strengthening with help, providing tayyibat, like rain that purified, hearts bound, steps firmed, like the Book explained with knowledge, guide and mercy, and like the noble Word fulfilled for those who patiently endured. The law states the favour remains until the people change what is in themselves, “ma bi-anfusihim,” the inward faith, the hearts that tremble, the trust, the establishing prayer, the best clothing of taqwa.\n\nThis principle explains why “Had the people of those societies been faithful and mindful We would have overwhelmed them with blessings from heaven and earth, but they disbelieved so We seized them.” The seizing for sins was preceded by self-change from belief to denial, from thankfulness to claiming “This is what we deserve,” from humble calling to taking faith as play.\n\nThe seal “Surely Allah is All-Hearing, All-Knowing” or “wa anna Allaha Samee‘un Aleem” recalls “And Allah is Fully Aware of what they do” when pagans left homes to be seen, and “Allah knows best what is hidden in the heart” when the dream showed them few, emphasizing He hears the whistling prayer and the “We hear but do not listen” claim, and knows the goodness whose absence makes Him not make them hear and even if hearing they would turn heedlessly.\n\nA common misunderstanding blames favour’s loss on external fate. The verse locates change inside: until they discontinue faith. For the believers fearing the trial that will not only affect wrongdoers and tempted to betray trusts for wealth that is fitnah, the law is both warning and hope: keep faith and the favour of shelter and help remains, as for the righteous community among Moses’ people that guides with truth, and for those who firmly abide by Scripture and establish prayer where reward is never discounted; discontinue faith and even the Sacred Mosque’s guardianship claim is void.",
  "54": "“That was the case with Pharaoh’s people and those before them—they all rejected the signs of their Lord, so We destroyed them for their sins and drowned Pharaoh’s people. They were all wrongdoers” repeats the wont with added detail to stress destruction as well as seizing. The previous verses described seizing for sins as the All-Powerful, severe, and the law that favour is not discontinued until faith is. This verse says “That was the case with Pharaoh’s people and those before them—they all rejected the signs of their Lord” or “kathaboo bi-ayati Rabbihim” they denied the signs of their Lord, like the staff-snake, shining hand, floods, locusts, lice, frogs, blood, the she-camel, the springs, the sea. The verb “kathaboo” is the same that made gates of heaven closed until camel passes needle and that made deeds vain, the denial that even if they see every sign they will not believe and prefer the crooked path.\n\nThe result “so We destroyed them for their sins and drowned Pharaoh’s people” or “fa-ahlaknahum bi-thunoobihim wa aghraqnaa ala Fir‘awn” distinguishes two forms: general destruction, or “ahlakna,” as with ’Ad uprooted, Thamud’s earthquake, Lot’s rain, Midian wiped, and specific drowning for Pharaoh’s people, the same drowning that answered the “If they desist past is forgiven, if they persist example of ancients” and that fulfilled “perhaps your Lord will destroy your enemy and make you successors to see what you will do.” The sea that was a road for the twelve springs becomes a grave for the heedless who were heedless of signs.\n\nThe verdict “They were all wrongdoers” or “wa kullun kanoo zalimeen” uses “zalimeen,” the unjust who place things where they do not belong, who place worship on lowing calves, who divide spoils unfairly, who take guardians besides Him, who betake wealth as test into betrayal, the same wrongdoing for which taste of burning is for what hands sent forth.\n\nThis wont, or “da’b,” illustrates the earlier “That was the wont, We never discount reward of those acting righteously” for the other path, but here the wont is wrong. A common error imagines Pharaoh’s drowning as isolated. The verse places it in the chain of “those before them” to warn the Quraysh who sought judgment and whose spending to hinder will become regret then defeat and driving into Hell, and to warn the spoils disputants that favour remains while faith remains, but wrongdoing, the wilful deaf and dumb worst beings, who have no goodness to make hear, invites the drowning of the accustomed wont.",
  "55": "“Indeed, the worst of all beings in the sight of Allah are those who persist in disbelief, never to have faith—” identifies the apex of wrongdoing just called “They were all wrongdoers,” continuing the Pharaoh wont. The previous verse ended with “They were all wrongdoers” after rejecting signs, being destroyed, and drowning. This verse grades that wrongdoing: “Indeed, the worst of all beings in the sight of Allah are those who persist in disbelief, never to have faith” or “inna sharra al-dawabbi inda Allahi allatheena kafaroo fa-hum la yu’minoon.” The phrase repeats the earlier “Indeed, the worst of all beings in the sight of Allah are the wilfully deaf and dumb, who do not understand” but now qualifies it with persistence: they persist in kufr, never to have faith, or “fa-hum la yu’minoon” they do not and will not believe, the same “even if they see every sign they will not believe, even if they see the Right Path they will not take it but the crooked they will,” and “Had Allah known any goodness He would have made them hear, but even if He had they would turn heedlessly.” Persistence is the infertile that hardly produces, the dog panting whether chased or left, the inheritor who says “We will be forgiven” and would seize again.\n\nThe emphasis “in the sight of Allah” shows the evaluation is not tribal “large numbers and arrogance are of no use” but divine weighing that is just, the weighing where light scales doom themselves, the hearing and knowing that was never absent. The worst are those whose hearts Allah stands between person and heart yet they refuse the life-giving call to respond when called to what gives life.\n\nThis verse links to the next specific description: namely those with whom you have entered into treaties but they violate them every time, not fearing consequences, showing persistence in disbelief takes treaty form in Medina’s context, where successive breakings by some Jewish tribes and pagan allies illustrated the wont.\n\nA common misunderstanding pities persistent disbelief as mere opinion. The Quran grades it as worst of beings because it is the root of fitnah that will not only affect wrongdoers, of hindering from the Path, of spending to hinder, of whistling prayer, of turning backs. For the believers told “Do not betray Allah, Messenger, and trusts knowingly” and “Wealth and children are only a test,” this warns that persisting in treaty betrayal for lowly gain makes the betrayer among the worst, whose evil plans Allah frustrates and whose forces no matter how numerous will not benefit because Allah is with the believers whose hearts tremble so perhaps they will be shown mercy when the Quran is recited and silently heard.",
  "56": "“˹namely˺ those with whom you ˹O Prophet˺ have entered into treaties, but they violate them every time, not fearing the consequences” specifies who the persisting worst are in the Medinan reality. The previous verse called the worst those who persist in disbelief never to have faith; this verse says namely those with whom you have treaties but they violate them every time. The phrase “those with whom you have entered into treaties” or “allatheena aahadta minhum” includes pacts made after the hijra with surrounding tribes and with the Jewish groups of Medina, whose covenants included not supporting an enemy against the Muslims. The qualifier “but they violate them every time” or “thumma yanqudoona ahdahum fi kulli marrah” shows repetition, not a single lapse, like the Sabbath town’s repeated fish tricks and the gate changers’ successive word changes and the lowly-gain inheritors who would seize again if similar gain came. “Fi kulli marrah,” every time, indicates habit, as earlier most were found not true to covenant and truly rebellious.\n\nThe closing “not fearing the consequences” or “wa hum la yattaqoon” lacks taqwa, the best clothing, the fertile thankfulness, the mindfulness that would have made them among those who firmly abide by Scripture and establish prayer, or at least not betray. Their lack of taqwa matches those turned away from signs for arrogance without right who even if they see every sign will not believe.\n\nThe verse connects the wont of Pharaoh who rejected signs and drowned to the treaty-breakers’ wont of violating pledges: both break the bound between person and Lord and between person and community, the same betrayal of trusts knowingly warned against, with wealth and children as fitnah test.\n\nA common error treats treaty violation as political flexibility. The Quran treats it as sign of persisting disbelief that makes them worst beings, because treaty is a covenant like the primordial “Am I not your Lord?” and like the mountain covenant to hold firmly. For believers told to obey and not dispute and to persevere with Allah who is with those who persevere, this warns that perseverance includes treaty fidelity, and that encountering such violators in battle, as the next verse says make a fearsome example so perhaps followers are deterred, is the just response to a persistent, fearless breach that frustrates peace that Allah prefers, as later “If the enemy inclines to peace, make peace and trust Allah, He is All-Hearing, All-Knowing” will show.",
  "57": "“If you ever encounter them in battle, make a fearsome example of them, so perhaps those who would follow them may be deterred” gives the battlefield instruction for the treaty violators persistently disbelieving never to have faith. The previous verse identified them as those with whom treaties were entered but violated every time without taqwa. This verse says “If you ever encounter them in battle, make a fearsome example of them” or “fa-imma tathqafannahum fil-harbi fa-sharrid bihim man khalfahum.” “Tathqafannahum” to find, to encounter, to take, as when the valley sides were near and far and the caravan below was taken by appointment, now the taking is of those who take as play. “Fa-sharrid,” make scattered, drive away, disperse, terrify so that others scatter, mirrors the dispersion of the Children of Israel through land in groups tested with prosperity and adversity, but here scattering is a deterrent punishment.\n\nThe purpose “so perhaps those who would follow them may be deterred” or “la‘allahum yathakkaroon” so perhaps those behind them will remember, the same “perhaps they will return, remember, abstain” that motivates every warning, from the Book as guide and mercy to the warner’s excuse to be free from blame and that they may abstain, to the wind-driven rain producing every fruit so perhaps you will be mindful. The fearsome example is thus not vengeance but deterrence, like showing the home of the rebellious that will be cast into Hell for the true losers, like the rod that devoured great magic to show illusions fail.\n\nThis instruction balances the later peace ethic “If the enemy inclines to peace, make peace and trust Allah” and the covenant ethic “If you see signs of betrayal, openly terminate the treaty,” showing that severity is for the encountered violator, not for the inclined to peace. It also balances the earlier “Attack the champions of disbelief who never honour oaths so perhaps they will desist” and “Fight until there is no persecution and devotion is entirely for Allah.”\n\nA common misunderstanding sees this as cruelty. The Quran frames it as measured deterrence that prevents the fitnah that will not only affect wrongdoers, the trial where a few treaty-breakers embolden many to violate, turning the land from set order to corruption, from twelve springs known by each tribe to the whistling prayer’s bed of Hell. The fearsome example scattering those behind preserves the shelter, help, and tayyibat given so perhaps you will be thankful, and keeps Allah’s planning, which frustrates evil plans and is best, as the clear standard to distinguish right from wrong for the mindful granted furqan.",
  "58": "“And if you ˹O Prophet˺ see signs of betrayal by a people, respond by openly terminating your treaty with them. Surely Allah does not like those who betray” extends the treaty ethic beyond the already encountered violators to the suspected. The previous verse said if you encounter treaty breakers in battle make a fearsome example to deter followers; this verse says if you anticipate breach before the sword is drawn, act transparently. The phrase “And if you see signs of betrayal by a people” or “wa imma takhafanna min qawmin khiyanatan” uses “takhafanna,” to fear, to sense, to notice signs, or “khiyanah,” betrayal, like the betrayal of Allah, Messenger, and trusts knowingly, the same khiyanah that the Sabbath town’s silent middle group tolerated. Signs could be mobilizations, secret messages to the enemy, or the spending to hinder from the Path.\n\nThe response “respond by openly terminating your treaty with them” or “fa-inbith ilaihim ala sawa’in” means throw, return the covenant upon them on equal footing, openly, so both sides know the treaty is ended with no ambiguity, mirroring the openness of “Hold firmly to that Scripture” and “Enter the city saying Absolve us and enter bowing,” where openness was required. “Ala sawa’in” equality ensures no treacherous surprise, the opposite of the deceivers who make misdeeds appealing and of Satan who says “I am by your side” then flees at the facing.\n\nThe reason “Surely Allah does not like those who betray” or “inna Allaha la yuhibbu al-kha’ineen” parallels “He does not like transgressors” when calling humbly and secretly and “He does not like those who betray” after the worst beings persist in disbelief. The Lord who loves the mindful, the good-doers whose mercy is close, and the righteous who firmly abide does not love betrayal, even against bet rayers, so the method must be honourable, not deceptive.\n\nThis verse links to the dispassionate rule for spoils and to the land belonging to Allah who grants to whom He chooses: betrayal in treaties is a form of betraying wealth and children as fitnah, seeking the near lowly gain. A common error argues that if betrayal is feared, preemptive deceit is permitted. The Quran commands the opposite: equality and openness, because the best planner is Allah, whose creation and command are His, whose angels make firm, whose rain purifies, and whose guidance via the most beautiful names is to keep away from those who abuse them and to stay with the truth-guiding, justice-establishing community whose reward is never discounted.",
  "59": "“Do not let those disbelievers think they are not within reach. They will have no escape” reassures the believers after the treaty instructions, closing the corridor where the persisting disbelievers, worst beings who violate every time, and the feared betrayers might imagine their manoeuvres have put them beyond reckoning. The phrase “Do not let those disbelievers think they are not within reach” or “wa la yahsabanna allatheena kafaroo sabaqoo” uses “sabaqoo,” to outstrip, to go ahead, to escape, as Pharaoh thought his constructions and establishments would outlast and as the Quraysh thought spending to hinder, hedging with large numbers, and fleeing Badr after the valley’s fewness would place them beyond the appointment. The verb “yahsabanna,” let them think, addresses the believers’ anxiety that the treaty breakers they chastised and those they openly ended treaties with might have slipped away.\n\nThe divine reply “They will have no escape” or “innahum la yu‘jizoon” means they will not frustrate, not incapacitate, the same “la yu‘jizoon” used for “Whatever you are promised will certainly come, you will have no escape” when the noble Word was fulfilled for those who endured, and for the Hour that knowledge is only with Allah but most do not know. The reach here is not only the battlefield’s “If you encounter them make a fearsome example” but the larger reach of the Lord whose creation and command are His, who created heavens and earth in six Days, subjects sun, moon, stars, sends winds with heavy clouds to lifeless land producing every fruit, and who dispersed the former peoples through the land in groups.\n\nThe verse also balances the previous “Allah is sufficient for you and for the believers who follow you” and “If the enemy is inclined to peace, make peace and trust Allah, He is All-Hearing, All-Knowing,” showing reach is not an invitation to chase beyond limits but a guarantee that no treaty betrayal escapes the weighing that will be just and the matters returned to Him for judgment.\n\nA common misunderstanding equates temporary escape after Badr with permanent immunity, as the Sabbath town’s breakers thought fish tricks would go unseen while He is never absent. The Quran says the slight delay is part of the gradual drawing in ways they cannot comprehend, with flawless planning that gives rope until the term that cannot be delayed, while the believers’ protection is the Protector who revealed the Book and protects the righteous. Trusting that there is no escape for the disbelievers frees the mindful from revengeful betrayal and keeps them firm, remembering, obeying, and persevering with Allah who is with those who persevere.",
  "60": "“Prepare against them what you ˹believers˺ can of ˹military˺ power and cavalry to deter Allah’s enemies and your enemies as well as other enemies unknown to you but known to Allah. Whatever you spend in the cause of Allah will be paid to you in full and you will not be wronged” turns from warning that disbelievers will not escape to the believers’ own preparation, the counterpart to the treaty violators’ spending to hinder. The command “Prepare against them what you believers can of military power and cavalry” or “wa a‘iddoo lahum ma istata‘tum min quwwatin wa min ribat al-khail” uses “quwwah,” strength, power, and “ribat al-khail,” tethered horses, cavalry ready, the deterrent force that makes the firm steps from rain and angelic reinforcement tangible in the world of means. “Ma istata‘tum,” what you can, mirrors “We never require of any soul more than what it can afford” after commanding full measure and weighing with justice, showing preparation is within capacity, not beyond, like establishing prayer and holding firmly to Scripture where reward is never discounted.\n\nThe purpose “to deter Allah’s enemies and your enemies as well as other enemies unknown to you but known to Allah” names three circles, the same three that surrounded Badr: the known enemy before you, the hypocrites with sickness in hearts who said believers are deluded, and the Satan who beautified and then deserted, the unknown devils who watch from where you cannot see them and who persistently plunge associates deeper without sparing effort. Deterrence, or “turhiboona,” to strike terror, to restrain, parallels the prior “make a fearsome example to deter followers,” but here by readiness rather than by punishment, keeping the land from corruption after it was set in order and keeping the Way not hindered.\n\nThe incentive “Whatever you spend in the cause of Allah will be paid to you in full and you will not be wronged” or “wa ma tunfiqoo min shay’in fi sabeeli Allahi yuwaffaa ilaikum wa antum la tuzlamoon” echoes “Whatever you spend in Allah’s cause will be paid in full and you will not be wronged” promised for the good, opposite to the spending to hinder that becomes regret then defeat and driving into Hell, and to the lowly gain seized claiming “We will be forgiven.” Spending for preparation is the infaq from what He provided, from tayyibat, from the honourable provision, whose tenfold remains, as earlier the fertile land produces abundantly by the Lord’s leave.\n\nA common error sees preparation as reliance on horses rather than trust. The Quran joins both: prepare with means as you stand firm and remember often, but know victory is only from Almighty, All-Wise and that Allah frustrates the evil plans, whose planning is flawless, and with whom the believers are.",
  "61": "“If the enemy is inclined towards peace, make peace with them. And put your trust in Allah. Indeed, He ˹alone˺ is the All-Hearing, All-Knowing” follows the command to prepare power and cavalry to deter enemies known and unknown, balancing readiness with mercy. The verse was revealed in a Badr aftermath where preparation and victory could tempt the believers to see every encounter as combat. The opening “If the enemy is inclined towards peace, make peace with them” or “wa in janahoo li-salmi fa-jnah laha” uses “janahoo,” to lean, to incline, like the incline of hearts that was just frustrated and like the inclined numbers and arrogance that were scattered. “Salm” is peace, wholeness, the same root as the Home of Peace and the greeting of the Heights. The command “fa-jnah laha” to incline to it shows the believers must mirror a genuine peace inclination, not exploit it, mirroring earlier “be gracious, enjoin right, and turn away from the ignorant” and “when you are inclined to peace, make peace.” This contrasts with those who lie in ambush on every road hindering the Way and making it seem crooked; peace is the straight path’s purpose.\n\nThe second command “And put your trust in Allah” or “wa tawakkal ala Allah” is the same trust praised as whoever puts trust in Allah, Allah is Almighty, All-Wise, and earlier “Whoever puts trust in Allah, Allah is Almighty, All-Wise.” Trust is required because peace may hide deception, as the next verse warns, but the believers’ trust is in the One who brought hearts together when all riches could not, who supported with help and believers, who is sufficient for the Prophet and followers.\n\nThe names “Indeed, He alone is the All-Hearing, All-Knowing” or “innahu huwa al-Samee‘u al-Aleem” close, as they closed when Allah knew what is hidden in hearts in the dream few, when He is never absent in giving a full account, and when He hears humble secret calling. He hears the enemy’s peace words and knows whether they are sincere, so the believers can incline without fear of hidden betrayal escaping His hearing.\n\nA common misunderstanding sees peace inclination as weakness after Badr. The verse shows it is obedience: preparation was for deterrence, not aggression for its own sake, and the Badr purpose was to establish truth and uproot persecution so devotion would be entirely for Allah. If peace achieves that without blood, it is preferred, preserving the creation that Allah would never change a favour until people change themselves and preserving the mercy close to good-doers.",
  "62": "“But if their intention is only to deceive you, then Allah is certainly sufficient for you. He is the One Who has supported you with His help and with the believers” completes the peace counsel with a guarantee against deception. The previous verse said if the enemy inclines to peace, incline, and trust Allah, the All-Hearing, All-Knowing. This verse acknowledges the fear that incline could be a trick: “But if their intention is only to deceive you, then Allah is certainly sufficient for you” or “wa in yureedo an yakhda‘ooka fa-inna hasbaka Allah.” The verb “yakhda‘oo” to deceive, to trick, echoes Satan’s beautification and cowering, the hypocrites and sick-hearted calling believers deluded, and the chiefs’ elegant words that incline hearts of those who disbelieve in the Hereafter. Deception in a peace offer would be like the treaty violators who violate every time without taqwa, the worst beings who persist in disbelief.\n\nThe assurance “Allah is certainly sufficient for you” or “fa-inna hasbaka Allahu” repeats the sufficiency promised to the Prophet and followers, “Allah is sufficient for you and for the believers who follow you,” the same sufficiency Moses found when Pharaoh said “We will kill sons” and he reassured “Seek help and be patient, the earth belongs to Allah.” Sufficiency means He will handle the deception, as He frustrated evil plans and made their large numbers of no use, as He cast horror and made steps firm, as He devoured great magic.\n\nThe proof of sufficiency “He is the One Who has supported you with His help and with the believers” or “huwa allathee ayyadaka bi-nasrihi wa bil-mu’mineen” recalls the specific help of a thousand angels murdifeen, drowsiness, rain, and the human help of the Ansar who sheltered and helped after being few and oppressed and then strengthened. The tafsir will explain in the next verse that bringing hearts together could not have been bought with all riches on earth, but Allah united them, showing support is not merely numbers.\n\nA common error thinks peace should be rejected because deception is possible. The Quran commands to incline if they incline, and leaves deception’s burden to Allah’s sufficiency. This balance protects the community from being drawn into the trial that will not only affect wrongdoers by refusing all peace, and from being lured into betraying trusts by accepting false peace without trust. It keeps the believers gracious yet discerning, enjoining right and turning from the ignorant, seeking refuge when Satan tempts, and remembering that the Protector who revealed the Book and protects the righteous is sufficient when voices deceive.",
  "63": "“He brought their hearts together. Had you spent all the riches in the earth, you could not have united their hearts. But Allah has united them. Indeed, He is Almighty, All-Wise” explains the humanity behind the divine sufficiency that supports with help and with believers. After saying if they intend to deceive, Allah is sufficient, He supported you with His help and with the believers, this verse reveals the inner miracle of that support. The phrase “He brought their hearts together” or “wa allafa baina quloobihim” describes the Ansar and Muhajirun, Aws and Khazraj, whose hearts had been enemies for generations, raiding, avenging, allied to different Jewish tribes, now united around the Lord of all worlds, the Same Lord whose throne was established after six Days and whose winds produce every fruit. The hearts, which Allah stands between person and heart and to Him all will be gathered, were the true battlefield, and their uniting is the greatest angelic help, more than a thousand angels, because united hearts make steps firm and disputes not weaken wind.\n\nThe counterfactual “Had you spent all the riches in the earth, you could not have united their hearts” or “law anfaqta ma fil-ardi jamee‘an ma allafta baina quloobihim” quantifies the impossibility: all riches, all spoils, all lowly gain successors seize claiming “We will be forgiven,” all the wealth disbelievers spend to hinder until it becomes regret, could not buy this union. The root “anfaqa” connects to “whatever you spend in Allah’s cause will be paid in full,” showing spending can deter enemies but cannot buy hearts; only the Lord who knows best what is hidden in the heart can.\n\nThe confirmation “But Allah has united them. Indeed, He is Almighty, All-Wise” or “wa lakinna Allaha allafa bainahum innahu Azeezun Hakeem” repeats the Almighty, All-Wise of victory only from Him, of the rain that firmed steps, of the dream few that spared dispute, of the trust that is not delusion. Might without wisdom would be Pharaoh’s “We will dominate,” wisdom without might would be a council that cannot deter; together they unite what riches cannot.\n\nA common misunderstanding attributes Medina’s unity to political skill alone. The verse insists the skill was divine, inviting those dividing the fifth for close relatives, orphans, the poor, and travellers to see that the best use of wealth is not to purchase loyalty but to preserve the unity Allah gave by obedience, not disputing, persevering with Allah who is with those who persevere, and by the gracious, firm, remembering way that keeps the trial from spreading.",
  "64": "“O Prophet! Allah is sufficient for you and for the believers who follow you” deepens the sufficiency promise after explaining heart-uniting that riches could not buy. The previous verse said if they intend to deceive, Allah is sufficient, supported with help and believers whose hearts He united. This verse personalizes sufficiency to the Prophet and his followers: “O Prophet! Allah is sufficient for you and for the believers who follow you” or “ya ayyuha al-nabiyyu hasbuka Allahu wa mani ittab‘aka mina al-mu’mineen.” The repetition of “hasbuka” is like “hasbaka Allah” for the peace deceivers, now broadened to include the following believers, the true believers whose hearts tremble, faith increases, trust is in the Lord, prayer is established, and donation from what He provided is given. Their following is not mere presence but the following of the light sent down to the unlettered Prophet, the firm abiding by Scripture where reward is never discounted.\n\nThe sufficiency includes the thousand angels, the drowsiness, the rain, the few-appearing-few, the horror cast, the necks and fingertips struck, the valley’s appointment, and the heart-union, but it is not limited to Badr’s past; it is the ongoing Protector described as “Indeed, my Protector is Allah Who revealed this Book, for He alone protects the righteous” and “What an excellent Protector and Helper.” The Prophet, who earlier left his home for a just cause while a group disliked it and disputed as if driven to death, now hears that the same Lord who brought them out, sheltered them, sheltered when few and oppressed, and provided tayyibat, remains sufficient for whatever they face, whether peace inclined to or deception hidden, whether captives and ransom to be judged, whether wealth and children are test.\n\nThe phrase also answers the anxiety that made the believers wish for the unarmed caravan: wishing for the easy because the difficult seemed insufficient without Allah. Knowing Allah is sufficient makes the difficult chosen for truth’s establishment bearable.\n\nA common error separates the Prophet’s sufficiency from the followers’, as if believers must find separate guarantees in numbers, spoils, or treaties that violate every time. The verse joins them: the believers who follow are included in the same hasbuka, provided they are the steadfast among whom twenty overcome two hundred when the burden was heavier and a hundred overcome two hundred now that it is lightened, the same steadfast with whom Allah is.",
  "65": "“O Prophet! Motivate the believers to fight. If there are twenty steadfast among you, they will overcome two hundred. And if there are one hundred of you, they will overcome one thousand of the disbelievers, for they are a people who do not comprehend” gives the combat ethic that flows from sufficiency. After saying Allah is sufficient for you and followers, the verse commands motivation, not compulsion, to stand firm and remember often so you may triumph. The phrase “Motivate the believers to fight” or “harridi al-mu’mineena ala al-qital” uses “tahreed” to urge, to excite, to make eager, like the winds ushering heavy clouds with glad tidings, the same encouragement Moses gave “Seek help and be patient” and Hud gave “Remember favours so you may be successful.” Motivation respects the heart Allah stands between, the heart whose goodness if known would be made to hear.\n\nThe ratio that follows “If there are twenty steadfast among you, they will overcome two hundred. And if there are one hundred of you, they will overcome one thousand of the disbelievers” sets a ten-to-one proportion, twenty to two hundred, hundred to thousand. “Steadfast,” or “sabiroon,” echoes “Allah is with those who persevere” and the patient inheritance of blessed lands, the steadfastness of magicians asking to be showered with perseverance to die as muslims, and the mountain’s hoped firmness. The tenfold matches the tenfold honourable provision for true believers and the tenfold reward for good deeds.\n\nThe reason “for they are a people who do not comprehend” or “bi-annahum qawmun la yafqahoon” identifies the enemy’s weakness: they do not understand, do not connect signs varied for the thankful, the way the worst deaf and dumb do not understand, the heedless who are like cattle. Lack of comprehension makes their numbers not benefit, as earlier “your forces no matter how numerous will not benefit, for Allah is with believers” and “Your large numbers and arrogance are of no use.” Their numbers are like the infertile land that hardly produces despite rain, while the steadfast twenty are fertile land producing abundantly by the Lord’s leave.\n\nA common misunderstanding reads the ratio as a permanent law rather than a Badr-specific strengthening. The next verse will lighten it, showing the Lord lightens burden knowing weakness, reducing to two-to-one, demonstrating that the same Almighty, All-Wise who makes the dream few and the appearance few also adjusts the law to capacity. For the spoils-concerned, motivation to fight with steadfast comprehension is the antidote to betraying trusts for wealth that is test, and to the fitnah that spares no wrongdoer, keeping the standard to distinguish right from wrong for the mindful.",
  "66": "“Now Allah has lightened your burden, for He knows that there is weakness in you. So if there are a hundred steadfast among you, they will overcome two hundred. And if there be one thousand, they will overcome two thousand, by Allah’s Will. And Allah is with the steadfast” shows the merciful adjustment after the ten-to-one standard. The previous verse set twenty to two hundred, hundred to thousand for those who do not comprehend. This verse says “Now Allah has lightened your burden, for He knows that there is weakness in you” or “al-aana khaffafa Allahu ankum wa alima anna feekum da‘fan.” “Khaffafa,” lightened, is like the raised mountain lightened when the Tablets were taken with strength, like the burden of the fifth made lawful after purification, like the mountain levelled to dust showing human inability to see Him. Knowing weakness is the All-Knowing who knows what is hidden in the heart, who knew the believers would falter if shown many in dream, who knows the goodness that makes hearing possible and the lack that makes even hearing turn away.\n\nThe new ratio “if there are a hundred steadfast among you, they will overcome two hundred. And if there be one thousand, they will overcome two thousand, by Allah’s Will” reduces from ten-to-one to two-to-one. “Bi-ithni Allah,” by Allah’s Will, or permission, shows victory is not arithmetic but divine leave, as earlier “It was not you who killed them but Allah who did so, nor was it you who threw but Allah who threw” and “Victory is only from Allah, Almighty, All-Wise” and “Whatever you spend will be paid in full.” The same wind that ushers heavy clouds can be light or strong; both are by His command.\n\nThe seal “And Allah is with the steadfast” or “wa Allahu ma‘a al-sabireen” repeats “Allah is with those who persevere,” the withness that makes the few firm, binds hearts, firms steps, casts horror, and unites hearts that riches could not. Steadfastness, or “sabr,” is the same steadfastness praised for the oppressed successors who endured, for the magicians’ prayer, for Moses’ counsel.\n\nA common error views the lightening as lowering ideals. The Quran shows it is mercy that keeps the standard to distinguish for the mindful attainable, ensuring the community that was sheltered when few and provided tayyibat can still be thankful without being crushed. For those dividing spoils and tempted to betray trusts for wealth that is fitnah, the message is to be steadfast within the lightened measure, trusting the Protector who revealed the Book and protects the righteous, whose help is sufficient whether twenty or a hundred.",
  "67": "“It is not fit for a prophet that he should take captives until he has thoroughly subdued the land. You ˹believers˺ settled with the fleeting gains of this world, while Allah’s aim ˹for you˺ is the Hereafter. Allah is Almighty, All-Wise” corrects the decision made at Badr regarding captives and ransom, the same spoils whose one-fifth was just detailed. After the ratios of steadfastness were lightened, this verse addresses the action already taken: taking seventy captives from Quraysh for ransom before the enemy was subdued. The phrase “It is not fit for a prophet that he should take captives until he has thoroughly subdued the land” or “ma kana li-nabiyyin an yakoona lahu asra hatta yuthkhina fil-ardi” uses “yuthkhina,” to make thoroughly subdued, to weaken the enemy’s power to hinder from the Path, like the uprooting of disbelievers and the cutting of their tail, like drowning Pharaoh’s people and the dreadful punishment for the Sabbath violators. The prophet’s fitness is measured by what establishes truth and wipes out falsehood even to the wicked’s dismay, not by what collects the near lowly gain.\n\nThe rebuke “You believers settled with the fleeting gains of this world, while Allah’s aim for you is the Hereafter” or “tureedoon arad al-dunya wa Allahu yureedu al-akhirah” names the motive behind the captives’ ransom: “arad al-dunya,” the fleeting goods of this world, the near, as earlier the lowly gain successors seized claiming “We will be forgiven” and the adornment brought forth for believers but exclusive in the Hereafter. Allah’s aim is the Hereafter, the elevated ranks, forgiveness, honourable provision, the Home far better for the mindful, the weighing just where heavy scales succeed.\n\nThe names “Allah is Almighty, All-Wise” or “wa Allahu Azeezun Hakeem” close, as with victory only from Him, heart-uniting, and dream fewness, showing the prophet’s error was not in intention but in preference within the ambition that Allah alone decides through His Messengers’ conveyance.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats ransom as purely merciful release. The verse shows timing matters: thorough subduing must precede captives, otherwise the fitnah that will not only affect wrongdoers spreads and the wealth-and-children test taints the beautiful test. The next verses’ decree and permission to enjoy what was taken as lawful and good after the prior decree show that even this misstep is handled with mercy, but the lesson remains: the mindful granted furqan must choose the Hereafter over the near, lest the near gain, like the whistling prayer, be tasted as punishment.",
  "68": "“Had it not been for a prior decree from Allah, you would have certainly been disciplined with a tremendous punishment for whatever ˹ransom˺ you have taken” softens the previous correction with a reminder of a protective decree, showing the severity that was spared. After saying it is not fit for a prophet to take captives until thoroughly subdued and that the believers settled for the fleeting gains while Allah aimed for the Hereafter, this verse says had it not been for a prior decree, or “lawla kitabun mina Allahi sabaqa,” a writing that preceded, you would have been touched by great punishment for what you took. The “kitab sabaqa,” prior decree, is understood by commentators as Allah’s prior permission for spoils to be lawful for this community, the prior writing that the noble Word would be fulfilled for those who endured, that the Hereafter’s better home is for the mindful, and that past sins would be forgiven if they desist. Without that decree, the ransom taken as “arad al-dunya” would have been the lowly gain that inherits Scripture and is seized while claiming “We will be forgiven” and then seized again, deserving severe, or “azeem,” punishment like the dreadful punishment for the Sabbath rebellious and the severing of hands and feet threatened.\n\nThe phrase “tremendous punishment” or “athabun azeem” matches the tremendous Day Noah feared and the severe punishment that will not be averted from the wicked, underscoring that the believers’ slip, though from the best of intend, was grave because it preferred near over Hereafter. The prior decree thus functions as the mountain raised like a cloud but not fallen, the drowsiness that gave serenity instead of horror, the rain that purified rather than drowned.\n\nThis verse connects to the law that Allah would never discontinue favour until people change faith and that He is All-Hearing, All-Knowing: the favour of spoils being made lawful was a change from earlier communities where spoils were not lawful, a favour written before their choice, so that even when they changed preference toward dunya, the favour’s prior decree cushioned them.\n\nA common error assumes that because they were not punished, the choice was correct. The verse says the absence of tremendous punishment is due to a prior decree, not to correctness, and that the decree itself is a mercy to be met with mindfulness, not with repeating the near-gain preference. The next verse’s permission to enjoy what was taken as lawful and good, with mindfulness and forgiveness, turns that prior decree into the explicit permission that transforms seizure into enjoyment without betrayal.",
  "69": "“Now enjoy what you have taken, for it is lawful and good. And be mindful of Allah. Surely Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful” lifts the weight after the prior decree spared the believers from tremendous punishment for the ransom. The opening “Now enjoy what you have taken, for it is lawful and good” or “fa-kuloo mimma ghanimtum halalan tayyiba” uses “kuloo,” eat, enjoy, the same eating permitted for garden fruits, cattle fit for labour, and twel ve springs’ tayyibat where they ate with thanks. “Halalan tayyiba,” lawful and good, is the opposite of the lowly gain seized while claiming “We will be forgiven,” the impure seized by successors, the fleeting goods, and the made-up shares for associates. Spoils are now declared halal tayyib by the prior decree, making them like the manna and quails where they were told “Eat from the good things We have provided,” not like the stone-prayer that sought punishment.\n\nThe command “And be mindful of Allah” or “wa ittaqoo Allah” is the same taqwa that is the best clothing, that makes the infertile produce, that gives furqan, that makes the Home of the Hereafter far better, the taqwa that the poor believers used to invoke morning and evening and that the Heights hoped to enter. Mindfulness after enjoyment prevents the new lawful gain from becoming the next fitnah that will not only affect wrongdoers, from becoming betrayal of trusts knowingly, and from becoming dispute that discourages and makes wind go.\n\nThe names “Surely Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful” or “inna Allaha Ghafoorun Raheem” repeat the mercy that encompassed the seventy after the earthquake their foolish did, the calf worshippers whose deeds became vain but who could repent and become believers to find Him All-Forgiving, Most Merciful, and the successorship test where hasanah in both worlds is written for those who shun evil, pay zakat, and believe. Forgiveness here covers the preference for near over Hereafter that prompted the captives, and mercy makes the spoils, like the fifth for orphans, poor, and travellers, a means of honourable provision rather than disgrace.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats lawful after pardon as unrestricted indulgence. The verse joins halal tayyib with ittaqoo, ensuring enjoyment remains within the just measure, the gracious enjoining of right, and the turning from the ignorant, so the trial of wealth and children, the fitnah of spoils, becomes the thankful remembrance of shelter when few and oppressed, and the elevation that remains is rank, forgiveness, and karim provision from the Lord who is All-Hearing, All-Knowing.",
  "70": "“O Prophet! Tell the captives in your custody, ‘If Allah finds goodness in your hearts, He will give you better than what has been taken from you, and forgive you. For Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful’” turns from the believers’ enjoyment to the captives’ future. The previous verse permitted enjoyment as halal tayyib with mindfulness, All-Forgiving, Most Merciful. Now the captives taken until the land was subdued are addressed. The opening “O Prophet! Tell the captives in your custody” or “ya ayyuha al-nabiyyu qul li-man fi aidikum mina al-asra” shows the Prophet as warner and bringer of good news, the same warner who motivates to fight and who is sufficient for believers. “If Allah finds goodness in your hearts, He will give you better than what has been taken from you, and forgive you” or “in ya‘lami Allahu fi quloobikum khairan yu’tikum khairan mimma ukhitha minkum wa yaghfir lakum” centres on khair, goodness, the same khair whose absence made Him not make them hear even if heard they would turn heedlessly, and whose presence makes fertile land produce abundantly, makes faith increase, makes remembrance constant. Goodness here includes inclination to truth, recognition of the unlettered Prophet whose description they find in Torah and Gospel, and desire for the guidance that relieves burdens.\n\nThe promise “He will give you better than what has been taken from you” replaces the fleeting goods of this world, the arad al-dunya and the fifth’s share, with better, like the tenfold honourable provision and the hasanah in both worlds written for the mindful, the better Home far better than lowly gain seized. The second promise “and forgive you” uses the same forgiveness the calf cohort needed after remorse lest they be losers and Moses sought for himself and brother as most merciful of the merciful, showing surrender can turn ransom loss into sin forgiveness, as with those who commit evil then repent and believe to find All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\n\nThe names “For Allah is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful” seal, as after eating lawful and good, emphasizing that captivity is not final damnation but a trial where Allah stands between person and heart and to Him all will be gathered, and where wealth and children are test.\n\nA common error views captives as merely ransom revenue. The verse views them as hearts where goodness may be found, inviting them to the same “We will be forgiven” not as false claim while seizing again but as true forgiveness after goodness found, guiding the community that was told to firmly abide by Scripture and establish prayer where reward is never discounted to treat captives with the gracious, just invitation rather than the whistling, hindering way.",
  "71": "“But if their intention is only to betray you ˹O Prophet˺, they sought to betray Allah before. But He gave you power over them. And Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise” balances the previous promise of better if goodness is found with the warning if betrayal remains. The verse says “But if their intention is only to betray you O Prophet, they sought to betray Allah before. But He gave you power over them” or “wa in yureedo khiyanataka fa-qad khanoo Allaha min qablu fa-amkana minkum.” The khiyanah, betrayal, echoes “Do not betray Allah and Messenger, nor betray trusts knowingly” and “If you see signs of betrayal, openly terminate on equal footing” and “Allah does not like betrayers.” Captives’ betrayal would be like the treaty violators who violate every time without taqwa, the worst beings who persist never to have faith, the Sabbath tricksters, the gate changers. The phrase “they sought to betray Allah before” links their present intent to prior betrayal of the covenant to speak only truth and to worship alone, the primordial “Am I not your Lord?” and the mountain covenant, and to the specific betrayal of supporting the enemy against the mindful while claiming to be guardians of the Sacred House.\n\nThe counter “But He gave you power over them” or “fa-amkana minkum” uses “amkana,” to give enablement, to make possible, the same enablement that made believers few over many, made hearts united when riches could not, made rain purify and angels firm steps, made truth prevail and illusions fail, made the sea a road and then a grave. Enablement is the other face of the fifth’s distribution and the valley’s appointment: Allah’s power, not the captives’ ransom, decided Badr.\n\nThe names “And Allah is All-Knowing, All-Wise” or “wa Allahu Aleemun Hakeem” close, as with the dream few where He knows best what is hidden in the heart and with the lightening of burden knowing weakness, and with victory only from Allmighty, All-Wise. He knows goodness in hearts versus betrayal intent, and His wisdom decides to give better and forgiveness or to give power over betrayers.\n\nA common misunderstanding thinks captivity’s outcome rests on the captor’s leniency alone. The verse says outcome rests on Allah’s enabling based on knowledge of betrayal past and present, inviting captives to choose the goodness that finds better and forgiveness rather than the betrayal whose power has already been shown to be frustrated, and inviting believers dividing spoils to trust the All-Knowing, All-Wise whose creation and command are His rather than betraying trusts for near gain.",
  "72": "“Those who believed, emigrated, and strived with their wealth and lives in the cause of Allah, as well as those who gave them shelter and help— they are truly guardians of one another. As for those who believed but did not emigrate, you have no obligations to them until they emigrate. But if they seek your help ˹against persecution˺ in faith, it is your obligation to help them, except against people bound with you in a treaty. Allah is All-Seeing of what you do” defines the new social order after the captives and spoils, organizing the community that was just promised help, heart-union, and suffisance. The first group “Those who believed, emigrated, and strived with their wealth and lives in the cause of Allah” are the Muhajirun, who left homes like the Prophet was brought out for a just cause, who strived with amwal and anfus as wealth and children test, and “as well as those who gave them shelter and help—they are truly guardians of one another” are the Ansar, who sheltered, or “awaw,” and helped, or “nasaroo,” after being few and oppressed then sheltered and strengthened with tashkuroon. Their guardianship, or “awliya,” contrasts with the treaty breakers whose guardianship is betrayal and with the devils who are allies of those who disbelieve. True guardianship is the fertile guardianship of the Protector who revealed the Book and protects the righteous, and of the believer who responds to what gives life.\n\nThe second group “As for those who believed but did not emigrate, you have no obligations to them until they emigrate” addresses Bedouin believers who stayed, lacking the trial that proves faith as the believers’ dispute as if driven to death proved, like those who inherit Scripture but do not firmly abide. No wilayah obligation means no mutual defence and spoil share until hijra completes the test.\n\nThe exception “But if they seek your help against persecution in faith, it is your obligation to help them, except against people bound with you in a treaty” balances the law of treaties and fitnah that will not only affect wrongdoers: help in faith is obligatory, but not by violating an equal-footing treaty openly terminated if betrayal is feared, because Allah does not like betrayers and loves the mindful who honour terms until end.\n\nThe seal “Allah is All-Seeing of what you do” repeats the “All-Seer” after the mountain, the “never absent” after the full account, the “encompassing” of what they do when leaving homes to be seen. A common error treats emigration as mere geography. The Quran shows it is commitment that creates wilayah, the earth belonging to Allah to grant to whom He chooses, so that help, spoils’ fifth, and deterrent preparation all serve a community where true guardianship is ranked by belief, emigration, striving, shelter, and help, not by lowly gain seized.",
  "73": "“As for the disbelievers, they are guardians of one another. And unless you ˹believers˺ act likewise, there will be great oppression and corruption in the land” gives the counterpart to the believers’ guardianship, defining the opposing alliance that makes Badr’s help necessary. The previous verse described Muhajirun and Ansar who guarded each other as true awliya, with exceptions for non-emigrants except when persecuted and treaty-bound. This verse says “As for the disbelievers, they are guardians of one another” or “wa allatheena kafaroo ba‘duhum awliya’u ba‘d” some of them are allies of others, like the earlier worst beings who persist, the treaty violators, the hinderers who spend to hinder and wage whistling prayer, the Satan who beautifies and deserts, the hypocrites with sickness who call believers deluded, all guardians of each other against the mindful. This is the same “wrongdoers are allies of one another because of misdeeds” and “devils are allies of those who disbelieve” and “We dispersed them in groups some righteous others less so” complexity.\n\nThe warning “And unless you believers act likewise, there will be great oppression and corruption in the land” or “illa taf‘aloohu takun fitnatun fil-ardi wa fasadun kabeer” uses “fitnah,” the same trial that will not only affect wrongdoers and that is wealth and children, now as great oppression, and “fasad kabeer,” great corruption, the same great corruption after the land was set in order that rain and angels and firm steps were sent to prevent. If believers do not guard each other as devils guard each other, the earth that belongs to Allah will be filled with the whistling, the calf lowing, the Sabbath fish tricks, the gate word changes, the market defrauding, the ambush on every road making the Way seem crooked—exactly what Badr was to uproot so devotion would be entirely for Allah.\n\nThe verse thus justifies the fifth, the deterrent preparation of power and horses to terrify enemies known and unknown, and the ratios of steadfastness, all as means to ensure wilayah of believers prevails over wilayah of disbelievers.\n\nA common misunderstanding praises neutral non-alignment between the two guardian-ships. The Quran says neutrality leaves fitnah and great fasad, as the silent middle of the Sabbath town was not praised as rescued. The fertile community must mirror the disbelievers’ mutual aid with its own, as the hearts that Allah united must remain united, firm, remembering often, obeying, not disputing, persevering with Allah who is with those who persevere, so the land’s order remains and the Hour’s tremendous surprise does not find oppression reigning because the mindful neglected to guard each other.",
  "74": "“Those who believed, migrated, and struggled in the cause of Allah, and those who gave ˹them˺ shelter and help, they are the true believers. They will have forgiveness and an honourable provision” repeats the guardianship portrait with its reward to seal the Badr surah’s ethic before the final inheritance rule. The previous verses warned unless believers act as mutual guardians there will be great oppression and corruption, and defined the two guardian-ships. This verse repeats “Those who believed, migrated, and struggled in the cause of Allah, and those who gave them shelter and help, they are the true believers” or “allatheena amanoo wa hajaroo wa jahadoo fi sabeeli Allaha wa allatheena awaw wa nasaroo ula’ika humu al-mu’minoon haqqa,” the same true belief, or “haqqa,” earlier defined by trembling hearts, increasing faith, trust, establishing prayer, and donating, the true belief that earns elevated ranks.\n\nThe reward “They will have forgiveness and an honourable provision” or “lahum maghfiratun wa rizqun kareem” repeats the honourable provision promised for true believers at the surah’s start, after the spoils’ fifth for orphans, poor, travellers, after the rain that purified and firm steps, after the thousand angels bushra. “Maghfirah” is the covering the calf worshippers sought when they realized they strayed, and that Moses sought for himself and his brother as most merciful, and that was promised to captives if goodness is found. “Rizq kareem” is the tayyibat provided when sheltered and strengthened so perhaps you would be thankful, now made honourable, generous, lasting, unlike the fleeting gains of this world the believers were told not to settle for and the lowly gain successors seize.\n\nThe repetition after the guardianship warning shows that honourable provision is not automatic with emigration alone but with the chain: belief plus emigration plus striving with wealth and lives as the test of amwal and awlad, plus shelter and help that is wilayah, the heart-union riches could not buy.\n\nA common error separates true belief from struggle and shelter. The verse joins them, as the earth that belongs to Allah and outcome for the righteous requires both striving and sheltering, both the staff thrown and the sea crossed, both the dream few and the double fewness. For the spoils dividers, the honourable provision redefines spoils: the fifth’s distribution is not the whole provision; the greater provision is the forgiveness and karim rizq that comes from being true believers who, when called to what gives life, respond while Allah stands between person and heart and to Him all will be gathered, and who, when they face an enemy, stand firm, remember often, obey, and persevere.",
  "75": "“And those who later believed, migrated, and struggled alongside you, they are also with you. But only blood relatives are now entitled to inherit from one another, as ordained by Allah. Surely Allah has ˹full˺ knowledge of everything” closes the guardianship order with a final inclusion and a boundary. The previous verse gave the core true believers—Muhajirun who believed, emigrated, struggled, and Ansar who sheltered and helped—their forgiveness and honourable provision. This verse adds the later comers: “And those who later believed, migrated, and struggled alongside you, they are also with you” or “wa allatheena amanoo min ba‘du wa hajaroo wa jahadoo ma‘akum fa-ula’ika minkum.” “Min ba‘du,” later, after Badr, after the spoils’ fitnah and the trial of wealth and children, still counts if the same belief, hijra, and jihad are made, showing succession remains open, like the hasanah written for this world and the next for those who shun evil, pay zakat, and believe and follow the unlettered Prophet, and like the green fruit produced from lifeless land when winds drive heavy clouds.\n\nThe boundary “But only blood relatives are now entitled to inherit from one another, as ordained by Allah” or “wa ulu al-arhami ba‘duhum awla bi-ba‘din fi Kitabi Allah” abrogates the earlier brotherhood inheritance based on faith alone where Muhajirun and Ansar inherited from each other, now restricting inheritance, or “irth,” to blood kin, or “ulu al-arham,” womb relatives, as ordained in Allah’s Book. This restores the primordial created-from-single-soul kinship within the new faith community, balancing the earlier “Those with whom you have treaties violate every time” by limiting treaty-like inheritance to its proper place. The spoils’ fifth remains for the community’s orphans, poor, travellers, but family inheritance returns to arham, preventing the confusion that faith abrogates natural ties or that near gain can be seized as inheritance without blood.\n\nThe seal “Surely Allah has full knowledge of everything” or “inna Allaha bi-kulli shay’in aleem” repeats the All-Knowing who knows what is hidden in hearts, what is between person and heart, who knows goodness that makes hearing possible, who knows best what is hidden, whose knowledge encompasses the fifth, the valley sides, the dream, the double fewness, the betrayals openly terminated, the spending that will be paid in full, and the angelic bushra. A common misunderstanding freezes the early brotherhood inheritance as permanent. The verse shows the Book explains with knowledge, varying lessons for the thankful, and can ordain a new rule that honors both the wilayah of believers that prevents great oppression and the arham that the mountain covenant to hold firmly and the remembrance when few and oppressed must also honor, keeping the earth Allah grants to whom He chooses orderly before the matters returned for judgment."
};
